I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for your attorney so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. Below is a focused, practical plan you can use at your meeting with Robert Kim and to prepare for the status conference in two months.

First — a few quick clarifying questions (so I can tailor this more precisely):
- Are there minor children of the marriage? (custody/visitation/support will change priorities)
- Is there a temporary order already in place (custody, support, possession of home, exclusive use)?
- Have you received a settlement offer already or any formal proposals from the respondent?
- Major assets to consider (home, retirement accounts, business, stock/options, significant debts)?
- Any safety or domestic violence concerns? (affects urgency and temporary orders)

If you prefer, I can draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for guidance before the status conference.

What the court/attorney will expect you to have and bring
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years where applicable):
  - Recent pay stubs, W-2s, 1040 tax returns
  - Bank statements (personal and joint)
  - Retirement account statements (401(k), IRA, pension)
  - Mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles
  - Credit card statements, loan statements, other debts
  - Business financials (if you or spouse own a business): balance sheets, profit/loss
  - Recent valuation reports or appraisals if available
- Household budget and monthly expenses
- Any pre-/post-nuptial agreements, prior separation agreements
- Communications supporting positions (texts, emails) — especially about finances, parenting, admissions
- Evidence relevant to custody (school records, schedules, third-party observations) if children involved
- Any police reports, medical records, or other safety-related documents
- Witness names and brief notes on what each would testify about
- Copies of any discovery already exchanged and court filings (complaint, answers, motions)

Specific questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped by topic)

1) Settlement vs. Trial — strategy and evaluation
- What is our realistic best-case and worst-case result at trial for each major issue (property division, alimony, child custody/support)?
- Based on the facts and evidence we have, how strong are our claims/defenses? Which issues are likely to be decided in our favor and which are risky?
- What is the likely settlement range you’d recommend? What would you consider an acceptable minimum (my reservation point)?
- What are the non-negotiable items (e.g., custody terms, keeping the house, certain assets)?
- Are there likely to be financial or tax consequences of settlement terms I should know about?
- How confidential would settlement terms be vs. a trial (public record)?

2) Costs, timeline, and resources
- What are the estimated fees and expenses to get to trial (your fees, experts, depositions, valuation, court costs)?
- What is the estimated timeline to trial if we don’t settle (discovery schedule, expert reports, pretrial)?
- Are there cost-effective alternatives (mediation, arbitration, settlement conference) you recommend?

3) Evidence & discovery
- What crucial evidence do we still need to obtain? (financials, valuations, communications, witness testimony)
- Do we need expert witnesses (forensic accountant, business valuation, child psychologist)? If so, estimated cost and timing?
- What discovery will the respondent likely request and how should we respond?
- Any urgent collection/preservation steps (for example, requesting a forensic copy of electronic records)?

4) Custody/support-specific (if children involved)
- Based on the facts, what custody/parenting time arrangement do you think is realistic?
- How is child support calculated here (Georgia guidelines) and what deviations are likely?
- How should we document parental involvement and routines to support our position?

5) Settlement mechanics and protections
- If we settle, what terms should be included to make the agreement enforceable and protective (spousal support duration/amount, QDRO for retirement, who keeps the house, tax allocation, debt division, parenting schedule, child support)?
- Do we need a QDRO or specialized documents for retirement account transfers? Who prepares them?
- Should settlement include confidentiality, non-disparagement, and release language?
- How are attorney fees and future disputes handled (attorney-fee shifting clauses, enforcement mechanisms)?

6) Trial readiness
- If we decide to go to trial, what needs to be completed before the status conference and by trial (depositions, experts, motions)?
- What is our witness list and order of proof — who will testify and what will they say?
- How will you present the strongest parts of our case at trial (theme, chronology, demonstrative exhibits)?
- What are the governing legal standards the judge will apply (Georgia equitable distribution, custody standard)?

7) Status conference-specific
- What should we ask the judge at the status conference and what should our proposed schedule look like (deadlines for discovery, expert reports, motion cutoffs, mediation)?
- Should we request a specific interim order, temporary hearing, or expedited relief before the status conference?
- Will the court require or encourage mediation/settlement conference? Are there local programs in Fulton County we should use?

Checklist to prepare before the status conference (what to complete in the next 2 months)
- Meet with Robert ASAP and give him the documents above.
- Define your objectives and absolute “deal-breakers.”
- Collect/organize financial docs (electronic and paper copies).
- Start or respond to discovery (interrogatories, requests for production) promptly.
- Decide whether to request mediation now or wait until after initial discovery.
- Identify and retain experts if needed (valuation, accountant, custody evaluator).
- Create a simple timeline of events and a folder of key communications.
- Prepare a short one-page summary of your case objectives to give to counsel.

Decision framework: when to accept a settlement vs. push for trial
- Accept settlement if:
  - The settlement falls within or above your reservation point and achieves your primary objectives;
  - The risks of trial (financial, time, uncertain outcome) outweigh potential benefits;
  - Settlement provides certainty, protects privacy, and includes strong enforcement language.
- Consider trial if:
  - Settlement offers are well below your reasonable expectation and the evidence strongly favors you;
  - There are important precedent or principle issues you must have resolved;
  - You can afford the time and cost and have a clear path to win on the crucial issues.
- Always compare: expected trial outcome (weighted by probability) minus expected costs/time vs. settlement net result.

Practical negotiation tips to discuss with Robert
- Ask for a clear written settlement range and the rationale (so you can evaluate offers).
- Consider staged concessions: hold firm on non-negotiables, be flexible on lesser items.
- Use mediation as a low-cost way to test the respondent’s bottom line while maintaining leverage.
- Set a deadline internally for settlement decisions so you don’t drift into last-minute panic.

What I can do next for you
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim with the key questions and request a meeting before the status conference.
- Create a printable checklist you can bring to your meeting and to the status conference.
- Help you draft a clear “objectives and bottom line” one-pager to give to your attorney.

Tell me which of those you want me to do (draft an email to Robert, create the checklist, or both). If you want the email, tell me whether you want it formal or brief and any specific points you want emphasized.

