I can help you get organized and ready for the status conference and for the settlement-vs.-trial decision. I’m not a lawyer, so this is general guidance to help you prepare questions and documents to discuss with Robert Kim at Kim & Associates and to be ready for the Civil Division in Fulton County.

1) Top-line checklist — documents to gather now
- Complaint / Answer / any pleadings already filed
- Court scheduling notices (status conference notice) and any prior orders
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years where available):
  - Tax returns (federal/state)
  - Recent pay stubs, employment verification, W-2s/1099s
  - Bank & brokerage statements
  - Retirement and pension statements (401k, IRAs, DB plans)
  - Mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles
  - Credit card and loan statements, debts
  - Business financials if a party owns a business (profit/loss, tax returns)
- Valuation reports (real estate, business appraisals) or quotes if you have them
- Monthly budget / living expenses (yours and any shared household expenses)
- Child-related records (school, medical, childcare costs) if custody/child support involved
- Communications and logs relevant to the case (texts, emails, call logs)
- Any evidence of abuse, threats, or protective orders (if relevant)
- Prior settlement offers or negotiation correspondence
- Witness list / contact info for people who can support your claims
- Any expert reports you already have or might want (forensic accountant, custody evaluator, appraiser)

2) Questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped for use in your meeting)
A. Case evaluation & likely outcomes
- Based on the facts, what are the strengths and weaknesses of my case?
- What outcomes do you think the court is most likely to grant on major issues (division of assets, alimony/spousal support, child custody/support)?
- How have similar cases in Fulton County typically been decided by this judge (if he/she is assigned)?

B. Settlement vs. trial — decision factors
- What settlement range would be reasonable given the facts and our objectives?
- What are the realistic risks of going to trial (probability of better/worse outcome)?
- What are the non-financial considerations (time, stress, privacy, relationship with children)?
- If we settle now, what terms should be non-negotiable? What can be deferred or revised later?
- If we push for trial, what must we prove and what will the other side likely argue?

C. Costs, timeline, and logistics
- Estimated costs to take the case to trial (attorney fees, experts, deposition/filing costs)?
- Estimated timeline to get to trial vs. time to reach a mediated settlement?
- Are there financial or procedural reasons to accept a faster settlement?

D. Discovery & evidence
- What specific discovery will you request (financial discovery, depositions, subpoenas)?
- What documents or witnesses do we need to strengthen our position?
- Do we need experts (e.g., forensic accountant, custody evaluator)? At what stage?

E. Settlement mechanics & protections
- How will settlement be documented to protect my future rights (releases, confidentiality, enforcement provisions)?
- Will the settlement be incorporated as a court order (so it’s enforceable)?
- What tax consequences or future modification risks should I consider (spousal support vs. lump sum, child support modifiability)?

F. Procedural / status-conference specific
- What should we file or bring to the status conference (case management statement, proposed schedule, financial affidavit, requests for mediation)?
- Are there deadlines the judge will set at the status conference that I should be aware of?
- Should we ask for a referral to mediation or a temporary orders hearing at the status conference?

G. Communication & negotiation strategy
- Do you recommend mediation? If so, when and with which mediator?
- What’s the negotiation strategy and who will do the negotiating?
- If an offer is made, how will you present options to me and how quickly do you need decisions?

3) How to evaluate any settlement offer (quick framework)
- Financial comparison: present value of offer vs. expected value at trial (include attorney fees and expert costs)
- Non-financial factors: time to resolution, emotional cost, publicity/privacy, impact on children
- Certainty vs. risk: guaranteed outcome now vs. uncertain potential at trial
- Flexibility & enforceability: are terms clear, enforceable, modifiable later?
- Tax & long-term implications: will the structure create tax exposure or future modification risk?

4) Prep to be ready for status conference (2-month timeline)
- Within 1 week: gather and send the documents above to Robert Kim; list witnesses and key dates/events
- Within 2–3 weeks: complete financial affidavit/forms the firm needs; identify any experts you might need
- Within 3–5 weeks: complete initial discovery responses and request the other party’s disclosures
- By 1 month: decide whether to propose mediation before the status conference and ask the attorney to schedule it if recommended
- 1–2 weeks before status conference: review proposed scheduling order / items to present to judge; confirm what you’ll say at conference

5) Practical meeting checklist — what you should tell Robert Kim in your next meeting
- Your ideal outcome and your minimum acceptable terms
- Key facts and documents you have and any you cannot access
- Sources of income and essential monthly expenses
- Any safety concerns or urgent issues involving children
- Deadline constraints (work, relocation, travel) that may affect scheduling

6) Suggested next step
- Would you like me to draft an email or a short agenda to send to Robert Kim that lists the documents you’ll bring and asks these specific questions before your status conference? I can draft a concise message or a one-page checklist for your meeting.

If you want, tell me:
- Which items you already have gathered,
- Which specific outcomes you care most about (money, custody, relocation, speed, privacy),
and I’ll tailor the list and a draft email or meeting script for Robert Kim.

