I can help you get organized. Below is a focused checklist of questions to ask Robert Kim and a list of documents/things to prepare before the status conference (and before deciding whether to accept a settlement or push for trial). I’ll also include a short email template you can send to your lawyer—tell me if you want me to draft or save it as a draft for you.

Important: this is general guidance to help your conversations with your attorney, not legal advice. Your attorney can apply these to the specifics of your case in Fulton County, GA.

Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)

Case status & timeline
- What is the purpose of the status conference the judge set, and what do you expect will be decided then?
- What deadlines (discovery, motions, disclosures, financial affidavits, expert reports) will the court set at or after the status conference?
- How long do you estimate the remainder of the process would take if we go to trial (scheduling, trial date, time estimate)?

Settlement vs. trial — case strength & likely outcomes
- Based on the facts and evidence so far, how strong is our case on each major issue (property division, alimony/spousal support, child custody/visitation, child support, debts)?
- What are the realistic ranges of outcomes we should expect at trial for each key issue?
- What are the justifications for recommending settlement vs. trial in this case?

Costs, risks, and benefits
- What are the estimated legal fees and other costs (experts, investigators, depositions) if we proceed to trial?
- What are the risks of going to trial (worst-case), and what are the likely benefits (best-case)?
- How would settlement affect future enforceability, modification, or appeals? Would any settlement terms bar or limit future claims?

Settlement specifics to evaluate
- If an offer is made, what specific terms should be included (payment schedule, lump-sum vs. periodic payments, tax treatment, division of retirement accounts, asset transfers, debt responsibility, parenting schedule, fees)?
- Should any settlement be made into a formal written agreement, stipulation, or consent order? Should it be incorporated into the final decree?
- Are there tax consequences or retirement/pension issues we need an accountant/financial advisor to review?
- Should we require confidentiality/non-disparagement clauses or mutual releases?

Evidence & trial readiness
- What specific evidence (documents, records, communications) will be critical at trial, and do we already have it? What’s missing?
- Do we need expert witnesses (valuations, forensic accountant, custody evaluator, psychologist)? What would they cost and how likely are they to help our case?
- Do we need depositions of the respondent or third parties? When should those happen?
- How long do you expect the trial to last and what is the likely calendar availability?

Children & custody-specific issues (if applicable)
- What custody/visitation arrangement is likely to be proposed by the other side and what arrangement should we seek?
- Will a custody evaluation or guardian ad litem be ordered? If so, what is the timeline/cost and how should we prepare?
- What documentation related to the children should I collect (school records, medical, schedules, communications)?

Temporary orders & immediate needs
- Should we seek or modify temporary orders (support, custody, exclusive use of the home) before the status conference?
- What interim protections (restraining order, temporary support) are advisable now?

Court procedure & judge preferences
- What are local rules and any judge-specific procedures or preferences in the Fulton County civil division that affect pacing or strategy?
- Does this judge favor settlement conferences, mediation, or early ADR?

Settlement negotiation strategy
- What is our BATNA (best alternative to a negotiated agreement) and reservation point (the minimum/maximum we would accept)?
- Should we present a written settlement proposal before the status conference or wait until after discovery?
- Would mediation or a private settlement conference with the judge be beneficial?

Costs/Payment structure of any settlement
- If settlement includes ongoing payments, how do we secure them (lien, security interest, wage garnishment, escrow)?
- If respondent might default, what enforcement mechanisms do you recommend including?

Appeals and long-term consequences
- If we settle now, what rights would we be giving up? Could either party later appeal or seek modification?
- If a trial verdict favors us, what are the typical timelines and costs for enforcement and appeal?

Practical next-steps to ask now
- What should I do in the next 2–4 weeks to prepare for the status conference?
- Can we have a pre-status-conference meeting to review our position and any settlement offers?
- What documents should I prioritize collecting and providing to you now?

Documents and items to gather (bring to your attorney / have ready)
Financial & asset documents
- Recent pay stubs, employment contract, and proof of income
- Last 3 years of tax returns (personal and any business)
- Bank statements (last 12–24 months)
- Retirement account statements, pension documents, 401(k), IRAs
- Mortgage statement, deed/title, property valuations/appraisals
- Vehicle titles/loan statements
- Business financials (if you or respondent own a business): balance sheets, profit/loss statements
- Credit card & consumer debt statements, loan agreements

Household & living costs
- Monthly budget, bills, proof of monthly expenses, childcare costs, health insurance premiums

Children / custody support evidence
- School records, medical records, activity schedules, daycare expenses, communication logs about parenting
- Any records of parenting time, travel, or extraordinary expenses

Communications & evidence
- Emails, texts, social media posts relevant to custody, income, assets, or the dispute
- Photos, videos, or other evidence tied to claims
- Police reports or protective orders, if applicable

Legal documents & prior agreements
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements, temporary orders, prior court orders, separation agreements
- Any prior settlement offers or communications regarding settlement

Other
- Witness names and contact info (people who can testify to relevant facts)
- Valuation reports, expert reports (if already obtained)

What to expect at the status conference (and what to ask for there)
- Expect the court to set a schedule for discovery, deadlines for disclosures, and possibly set dates for mediation or settlement conferences.
- Ask your attorney to request specific discovery deadlines or a scheduling order that fits your needs.
- If you need temporary relief (support, exclusive occupation), ask whether to file a motion before the status conference.
- Ask the attorney about requesting a settlement conference or mediation before substantial discovery if that’s desirable.

Suggested priority list (what to ask first in your next conversation with Robert Kim)
1. What is our overall recommendation: pursue settlement now, attempt mediation, or prepare for trial? Why?
2. What immediate tasks should I complete in the next 2–4 weeks?
3. What documents do you need from me first (top 5)?
4. Do you recommend an early valuation or expert? If so, which type and estimated cost?
5. Should we plan for mediation before or after discovery?

