I can help you get organized. Below is a focused checklist of questions and items to take to your meeting with Robert Kim, plus a short decision framework to help you evaluate whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. If you want, I can also draft an email to Robert with these questions or make a one-page printable checklist for the status conference.

Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)

1) Case status, timeline, and process
- What should I expect at the Fulton County civil division status conference in two months? Will the judge set a timetable, require filings, or set a trial date?
- Are there required filings or a status report I need to prepare before the conference?
- Are there mandatory ADR steps (mediation, settlement conference) in this court and when will those happen?
- What are the next procedural milestones and deadlines we should prepare for?

2) Strengths, weaknesses, and likely outcomes
- What are the strongest facts or pieces of evidence in my favor? What are the weaknesses or vulnerabilities?
- Based on your experience, what are the realistic outcome ranges for: property division, alimony/spousal support, child custody/time-sharing, and child support?
- What is your assessment of our probability of getting a better result at trial vs. in settlement negotiations?

3) Settlement-specific questions
- If an offer is presented, how should I evaluate whether it’s “fair”? What specific financial, parenting, and other terms should I insist on?
- Can you run numbers that show the long-term financial impact of the offer (present value of support, retirement splits, tax implications, debt allocation)?
- What non-financial terms matter (e.g., confidentiality, non-disparagement, custody schedules, relocation restrictions, decision-making authority)?
- If we accept, what protections or enforcement mechanisms should be included (e.g., security for payments, penalties for breach)?

4) Trial-specific questions
- If we go to trial, what evidence and witnesses will we need (e.g., financial expert, valuation expert, custody evaluator)?
- What is the estimated timeline to trial and how long will a trial likely last?
- What are estimated attorney fees, expert costs, and other out-of-pocket costs if we go to trial?
- What are risks associated with trial (unpredictability, publicity, emotional cost, possibility of appeal)?

5) Financial discovery and documentation
- What specific documents should I gather and bring (see list below)?
- Are there particular financial red flags or hidden transfers we should investigate (gifted assets, transfers to relatives, business valuations)?
- Do you recommend subpoenas, forensic accounting, or temporary restraining orders for assets/savings?

6) Child-related issues (if applicable)
- For custody/time-sharing: what factors will the court consider in Fulton County? What evidence do we need to support our parenting position?
- How is child support likely to be calculated here? Should we obtain a parenting plan proposal and income/expense schedule now?
- If relocation might become an issue, what protections should be in place in a settlement?

7) Costs, fees, and practicalities
- What are your current fee estimates for getting to trial vs completing a settlement?
- Can we negotiate attorney-fee provisions as part of a settlement (who pays fees if one side breaches)?
- Are there temporary orders we should pursue now (temporary support, temporary custody, exclusive use of the home)?

8) Strategic questions to ask yourself (with attorney’s input)
- What is my bottom line (monetary and custody) and my “walk away” point?
- What are my priorities (e.g., cash now vs retirement assets, custody stability vs more time-sharing, privacy)?
- How long am I willing to litigate (emotionally and financially) to try to get a better result?

Documents to gather and bring to your attorney meeting
- Marriage certificate, any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements
- Pleadings and court filings already served or filed
- Recent pay stubs, W-2s, tax returns (last 2–3 years)
- Bank statements, investment account statements, retirement account statements
- Mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles, loan documents, credit card statements
- Business records (if either spouse owns a business): profit & loss, balance sheet, tax returns
- Records of transfers or gifts during the marriage
- Bills, household monthly expense worksheets
- Insurance policies, beneficiary designations
- Communications or messages relevant to case (if relevant and admissible)
- Records related to children (school, daycare, medical, extracurricular costs)
- Any police reports, restraining orders, or prior family-court orders

How to evaluate an offered settlement (framework)
- Compare the settlement to your BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement): estimate best realistic trial outcome and probability.
- Quantify financial tradeoffs: up-front lump sum vs streams of support; present value of future payments; tax consequences.
- Add non-financial considerations: certainty/privacy, speed, emotional cost, child stability.
- Consider enforceability and protections in the agreement (security, contempt procedures, payment mechanisms).
- Ask your attorney to run 2–3 scenario projections (best reasonable, most likely, worst reasonable) with estimated costs.

Status-conference–specific checklist (what to have ready)
- Proposed scheduling order (if your lawyer recommends)
- Summary of outstanding discovery and what you need to collect
- Any proposed motions (temporary relief) you want the judge to consider
- Contact info for witnesses or experts you may call
- A short statement of whether you’re open to mediation and when

Practical tips for the meeting with Robert Kim
- Tell him your priorities up front (what you must have, what you can compromise on).
- Ask for written scenarios and numbers you can review at home.
- Ask him to set a recommended timeline for settlement negotiations vs preparing for trial given the two-month status conference.
- Request a realistic range of likely court outcomes and a written summary of pros/cons for settlement vs trial.
- Ask for a plain-English, one-page summary of the proposed next steps and timeline after your meeting.

If you want help now
- I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for a meeting before the status conference.
- I can turn this into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your attorney.
- I can also help you prioritize which documents to gather first.

Which of those would you like me to do next — draft an email to Robert, make a printable checklist, or something else?

