Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and ready for the status conference and your meeting with Robert Kim. Below is a practical, prioritized set of things to do and specific questions to ask your attorney about whether to accept a settlement or push for trial, plus a checklist of documents and a decision framework you can use to weigh settlement offers.

Quick note: I’m not your lawyer. This is guidance to help you prepare questions and make an informed discussion with Robert Kim.

1) Immediate priorities (before the status conference)
- Gather key financial and custody documents (see checklist below).
- Ask your attorney whether any temporary orders (support, custody, exclusive use of house, restraining orders) should be requested before the status conference.
- Confirm the status conference date/time, what the court expects you to file beforehand (case management statement, disclosures, proposed scheduling order), and any filing deadlines for the civil division in Fulton County.

2) Document checklist to bring to your attorney and to the status conference
Financial
- Most recent 2–3 years federal tax returns (with schedules)
- Recent pay stubs (last 3–6 months)
- Bank statements (last 6–12 months) for all accounts
- Investment account statements, brokerage, brokerage history
- Retirement account statements (401(k), IRAs, pensions)
- Mortgage statements, deed/title, property appraisals
- Vehicle titles, loan statements
- Credit card and consumer debt statements
- Business financials (if you or respondent own a business): profit & loss, balance sheet, tax filings
- Recent valuation or request for business valuation if needed
- Insurance policies (life, health, disability, homeowner)
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements

Custody / Children (if applicable)
- Child’s school records, daycare records, IEPs
- A parenting calendar or timeline of schedules, exchanges, missed parenting time
- Texts/emails relevant to custody, safety, or parenting
- Any police reports, CPS records or other safety documentation

Other
- Communications (email, texts) that bear on division of assets, misconduct, or agreements
- Any previously proposed settlement offers and signed agreements
- Court filings already exchanged and any discovery responses you have
- Witness names and contact details (friends, family, professionals)
- Evidence you think is critical (photos, recordings, receipts)

3) Specific questions to ask Robert Kim — grouped and ready to use
Case assessment & realistic outcomes
- Given the facts of my case and local practice in Fulton County, what outcomes should I realistically expect on the key issues (property division, alimony, child custody/support)?
- What are the strongest and weakest legal points we have? What evidence do we still need?
- Is a bench trial or jury possible/typical here? (Most family/divorce matters are decided by a judge—ask to confirm.)

Settlement strategy & evaluating offers
- What is my “best alternative to a negotiated agreement” (BATNA)? In other words, what do you expect the court would likely order if we go to trial?
- What is a reasonable settlement range for each key issue (property split, alimony, custody, support)?
- What immediate settlement red flags should I avoid (e.g., vague release language, leaving pension division unresolved, tax pitfalls)?
- How should we structure a settlement to be enforceable (e.g., QDRO for retirement, clear timelines, remedies for breach)?
- Are there confidentiality, non-disparagement, or relocation clauses you recommend?

Costs, timing & risks
- What are the estimated costs (attorney fees, experts, valuation, custody evaluators) for proceeding to trial vs completing a negotiated settlement?
- How long will it likely take to reach trial? How many court appearances and days of trial would you expect?
- What are the principal risks of trial (uncertainty of judge’s view, evidentiary rulings, appeals, likelihood of partial victories)?

Discovery & evidence
- What discovery remains essential (depositions, subpoenas for bank/business records, forensic accounting)?
- Should we hire experts (forensic accountant, business valuator, custody evaluator)? If so, when?
- Do we need preservation letters or emergency document subpoenas?

Settlement mechanics & legal details to confirm
- If I accept an offer, how will property be transferred and what paperwork is required (deeds, QDROs, mortgage refinances)?
- How are taxes handled for any settlement payments or asset transfers?
- Are there spousal support/alimony tax consequences I should know about?
- What language should be included to allow future modification (especially for child support/custody) and to protect me from future claims?

Trial preparation (if we choose trial)
- What is our trial timeline and what can I do now to prepare?
- What witnesses and exhibits will we need, and who will prepare them?
- What motions (motions in limine, summary judgment, temporary relief) should we consider filing before trial?
- How many days do you estimate the trial will last and what is the courtroom process in Fulton County civil division?

Settlement negotiation tactics & authority
- Who will negotiate? Will you handle all settlement communications or do you want me to be involved?
- What is your recommended walk-away number/terms? (Ask the attorney to help set a bottom line.)
- Are we willing to use mediation or private settlement conferences? If yes, when should mediation occur?
- If the respondent makes a low-ball offer, what is an appropriate counter-offer strategy?

Enforcement & long-term considerations
- How will the settlement be enforced if the respondent fails to comply (contempt proceedings, garnishment)?
- How easy or difficult will it be to modify future orders (custody, support) if circumstances change?
- Are there business succession or retirement issues that require special structuring?

4) Decision framework — how to decide whether to settle or go to trial
- Estimate the likely trial outcome: define the “expected value” for each major issue (e.g., property share, monthly alimony/support).
- Subtract estimated trial costs (attorney fees, experts) and add a risk premium for uncertainty (what you’d accept for certainty instead of risk).
- Compare net expected trial value to best settlement offers. If settlement >= expected trial outcome minus costs and risk, settlement often makes sense.
- Factor in non-financial items: time, emotional stress, impact on children, need for confidentiality, willingness to risk an unfavorable judge ruling.
- Consider whether a settlement can include protections that a trial cannot (confidentiality, specific enforcement terms).

5) Practical checklist for your meeting with Robert Kim
- Give him the document checklist above, organized and labeled.
- Ask him to prepare a short written case assessment: likely outcomes, strengths/weaknesses, recommended path.
- Ask him to propose a negotiation strategy and a settlement “floor” and “target.”
- Ask for a timeline with milestones to the status conference and any filings required before then.
- Decide whether you want him to prepare a one-page summary you can use at mediation or settlement talks.

6) Court / local practicalities to confirm with counsel
- What does the Fulton County civil division typically require at status conferences (case management statement, proposed deadlines, ADR/mediation attendance)?
- Are there local rules or judge-specific preferences we should be aware of? (Ask him to check the assigned judge’s standing orders.)
- Will the court require a financial affidavit or temporary relief filing by a certain date?

7) Sample phrasing you can use in the meeting
- “Based on the current evidence, what is your best estimate of my likely outcome at trial for property division and custody?”
- “Can you give me a bottom-line settlement number and a target settlement number, and explain how you arrived at them?”
- “What is the estimated total cost to take this to trial, including experts, and how does that affect our settlement calculus?”
- “If we accept a settlement, how will enforcement and future modification be handled?”

8) Would you like me to...
- Draft a one-page “issues and priorities” summary you can bring to your attorney or to mediation?
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing what you want to discuss at your next meeting and asking the key questions above?
- Create a checklist you can tick off before the status conference?

If you want, tell me which of the above you’d like me to draft (one-page summary or email to Robert Kim), and provide any details you want included (specific settlement offers you’ve been given, presence/ages of children, big assets to focus on, any urgent safety issues).

