I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Below I’ve put together (1) a focused list of the most important questions to ask Robert Kim about whether to accept a settlement or push for trial, (2) the documents and evidence you should gather before the status conference, (3) a simple decision framework to evaluate any settlement offer, and (4) what to be ready to say/ask at the status conference in two months. Tell me if you want me to draft an email or meeting agenda for your lawyer, or a printable checklist for the conference.

1) High‑priority questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
- What is our realistic best-case, worst-case, and most-likely outcome at trial on the key issues (property division, spousal support, child support, custody/parenting time, attorney’s fees)?  
- Based on the evidence we have, what are the strengths and weaknesses of our case? Which issues are most likely to sway a judge?  
- What are the likely costs (attorney fees, expert fees, court costs) and time to prepare for and go to trial? How would that compare to the financial terms of the current settlement offer?  
- What is the estimated probability (percentage) that we would obtain a favorable outcome at trial on each major issue?  
- Can you run an “expected value” comparison for the offer (likely award x probability minus costs)?  
- What are the non‑financial costs of trial (stress, time, public record, impact on children, business disruption)?  
- If we accept a settlement, what exactly will the settlement include and exclude? (Ask for a draft to review.)  
- Are there tax consequences I need to know about for any settlement items (e.g., alimony, property sale, retirement transfers)?  
- Will a settlement be fully enforceable? What enforcement remedies and timelines would be included (attorney’s fee shifting, contempt, security)?  
- If we reject the offer and go to trial, what pretrial steps and deadlines (discovery, depositions, expert reports, motions in limine) must we meet before the status conference?  
- Do we need any experts (forensic accountant, appraiser, custody evaluator, psychologist)? If so, when should they be retained and what will they cost?  
- Is mediation or neutral evaluation required or likely to be productive? If we mediate, what settlement floor/ceiling should I accept?  
- Are there any jurisdictional/local rules in Fulton County (or the specific civil division) that affect timeline or procedures I should know about?  
- If children are involved: how likely is a judge to order supervised visitation, a custody evaluation, or a parenting coordinator?  
- If there is any history of domestic violence, how will that affect settlement and trial strategy?  
- What are the risks of adverse precedent or future modification if we accept certain settlement terms?  
- If the respondent refuses settlement and we win at trial, what is the likelihood of collecting the judgment?  
- If I decide to accept, can you negotiate protective language (confidentiality, non‑disparagement, future modification limits)?

2) Documents and evidence to gather and bring to the status conference / for your attorney’s review
Financial documentation
- Last 3–5 years of federal tax returns (personal and business if applicable)  
- Recent paystubs (3–6 months), W‑2s, 1099s, profit & loss statements for business owners  
- Bank statements, investment/brokerage statements, retirement account statements (IRAs, 401(k), pensions)  
- Mortgage statements, deed/title documents, car titles, lease agreements, vehicle loan docs  
- Credit card statements, loan statements, outstanding debts  
- Recent appraisals, business valuations, or offers to buy property  
- Corporate financials, tax returns and K‑1s if a business is involved

Parenting / custody
- Children’s school records, schedules, medical records if relevant  
- Calendar of parenting time, childcare costs, daycare receipts  
- Any communications relevant to custody (texts, emails, social media) — label by date

Communications & supporting evidence
- All pleadings and court filings to date (complaint, answer, temporary orders)  
- Copies of any settlement offers in writing, mediation statements, or correspondence  
- Photos, videos, witness contact info, police reports, protective orders (if any)  
- Documentation showing hidden assets, transfers, or suspicious transactions (if suspected)

3) Decision framework: how to evaluate a settlement offer
- Identify the key issues the offer resolves (division of assets, alimony, child support, custody, debts, attorneys’ fees).  
- For each issue estimate: (a) likely dollar value (or custody outcome), (b) probability of winning at trial, (c) trial costs to you.  
- Compute a rough “expected value” = sum over issues of (value × probability) − estimated costs. Compare to the settlement numeric value.  
- Factor in non-monetary considerations: confidential record, speed, emotional cost, disruption, certainty, enforceability.  
- Consider staged or conditional settlement terms: e.g., lump sum now vs structured payments, escrow, security for payments, penalties for noncompliance.  
- Set a clear “bottom line” (walk‑away point) and a preferred outcome; tell your attorney to negotiate within that band.

4) What to prepare for the status conference (two months away)
- Ask Robert what the judge typically expects at a status conference in that Fulton County civil division (scheduling order, discovery deadlines, setting trial date, referral to mediation).  
- Prepare a proposed schedule: discovery timeline, expert disclosure dates, mediation date, pretrial motions deadline, and possible trial date windows. Ask your lawyer to file it if appropriate.  
- Confirm whether any temporary orders (custody/support/possession of assets) need to be requested or renewed before the conference.  
- Make a summary packet for your attorney: chronology of events, list of assets/debts, list of witnesses, and key documents. Keep a short one‑page “case facts” sheet for the judge/attorney.  
- Be ready to discuss settlement posture: whether you’re open to mediation and reasonable timelines for negotiations.

5) Practical negotiation and settlement items to check
- Payment terms: lump sum vs installments; security (escrow, lien on property, wage garnishment)  
- Division of retirement accounts: Qualified Domestic Relations Order (QDRO) language and process  
- Tax allocation and who bears tax liabilities for asset sales  
- Parenting schedule specifics, holidays, decision‑making authority, healthcare and education expense splits  
- Spousal support amount, duration, termination triggers, rehabilitation clauses, modification provisions  
- Confidentiality, non‑disparagement, mutual releases, and scope of release (what claims are waived)  
- Attorney’s fees and costs clause for enforcement and breach remedies

6) Questions I should ask you now (so I can help more specifically)
- Are there children involved? If so, ages and main custody concerns?  
- Rough idea of the marital estate complexity (real estate, business, retirement, separate property claims)?  
- Any history of domestic violence or restraining orders?  
- Do you already have a written settlement offer from the respondent (or a mediator)?  
- Do you want me to draft: (a) an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for his assessment, (b) a one‑page status conference agenda, or (c) a printable checklist you can bring to the meeting?

7) Offer / next steps I can do for you
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim summarizing the questions above and asking for his assessment and recommended next steps (I will not send it without your approval).  
- Create a one‑page “meeting script” you can use in the conference to make sure all points are covered.  
- Produce a numeric worksheet to compare settlement offers to expected trial value (I can populate it with your numbers if you want).  

If you want, tell me which of the follow‑ups you prefer (draft email to Robert, one‑page agenda, or the comparison worksheet) and provide any details you’re comfortable sharing (children, types of assets, whether a written offer exists). I will not send anything to anyone unless you explicitly ask me to send it.

