--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 6406 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist and set of questions to bring to your meeting with Robert Kim so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or push for trial, plus what to have ready for the status conference in two months. If you’d like, I can draft an email or a printable one-page list you can send or bring to the attorney.

1) Quick facts to confirm with your attorney (bring these to the meeting)
- Parties, case number, court name (confirm exactly which Fulton County court and division handling the divorce).
- Status conference date and any scheduling/order deadlines you’ve been given.
- Any temporary orders currently in place (custody, support, use of home, etc.).

2) Documents to bring / prepare
- Last 2–3 years of tax returns (with schedules).
- Recent pay stubs and proof of income (last 3–6 months).
- Bank, investment, retirement account statements.
- Mortgage and deed documents, titles, vehicle registrations.
- Credit card and loan statements, debts.
- Business financials (if either party owns a business).
- Retirement account summaries, pension statements.
- Insurance policies (health, life, homeowner).
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements or written agreements.
- Relevant communications (texts, emails) related to custody, support, finances.
- Parenting/time logs, incident logs (if custody dispute).
- Valuations, appraisals, bills, receipts for major assets or expenses.
- Any settlement offers previously exchanged (written or summarized).

3) Core questions to ask about settlement vs trial
- Based on the facts and documents I’ve given you, what range of settlement outcomes should I reasonably expect (for division of assets, alimony, child support, custody)?
- What is our realistic chance of getting a better result at trial? What is the worst realistic result at trial?
- What are the main strengths and weaknesses of my case (evidence, witnesses, legal arguments)?
- What specific facts or evidence are likely to be decisive at trial?
- What are the likely costs of going to trial (legal fees, expert witnesses, other expenses)? Estimate total dollar range and timeline.
- How long would it likely take to get to trial in Fulton County (including discovery, motions, scheduling)?
- Are there mandatory steps before trial (mediation, parenting classes, disclosures) in this court? Will the status conference trigger deadlines?
- If we accept a settlement, how will enforcement work? Are there risks that the respondent won’t comply? How do we protect against that?
- What are the tax consequences or other long-term impacts (retirement splitting, Social Security, business valuation)?
- How will a settlement affect confidentiality vs the public nature of a trial?
- If custody is involved: what factors would the court weigh most heavily here? How might the judge rule on custody/visitation?
- How likely is the court to award attorneys’ fees to either side?
- If we decline a settlement and lose at trial, can we appeal? What are the practical chances and costs of appeal?
- What is your recommendation right now and why? What threshold would you use to recommend accepting an offer (e.g., minimum financial amount or custody terms)?

4) Status-conference specific questions (two months away)
- What will the judge expect at the status conference (proposed schedule, discovery deadlines, referrals to mediation)?
- Are we required to file any reports, disclosure statements, or case-management forms before the status conference?
- Should we have a settlement proposal ready by then (or a settlement range)?
- What immediate steps should we take between now and the status conference to strengthen our position?
- Do you recommend starting discovery (document requests, interrogatories, depositions) now? Which items should we prioritize?
- Will the court set a trial date at the status conference or issue a scheduling order?

5) Strategy and decision-framework to discuss with your attorney
- Define your priorities (e.g., preserve custody arrangement, maximize financial recovery, speed and privacy) and ask the attorney to map outcomes to those priorities.
- Ask the attorney to provide a “bottom line” — a written best-case / likely / worst-case scenario and a recommended settlement threshold.
- Agree on triggers for accepting an offer (e.g., if settlement ≥ X or if custody terms include Y).
- Ask how the attorney will negotiate — timeline, who makes offers, whether to use mediation/neutral evaluator.

6) Practical items to request from your attorney
- A short, written summary of the options and recommended next steps after your meeting.
- A realistic cost estimate (billing rate, projected hours, expected outside costs like experts).
- A timeline showing critical dates and deadlines up to and after the status conference.
- Specific list of what you should do between now and the conference (document collection, communications to avoid, temporary arrangements to consider).

7) Emotional and non-legal considerations to weigh (bring these up)
- Your tolerance for time, stress, and publicity of trial.
- Financial ability to pay continued legal fees if the case goes to trial.
- Impact on children, work, mental health — factor these into the decision.

8) Sample decision checklist you can use in the moment
- Is the settlement at least equal to the attorney’s “likely” outcome at trial after costs? (Yes/No)
- Does the settlement secure the custody/parenting terms I need? (Yes/No)
- Are there enforceable protections (e.g., QDRO for retirement, security for payments)? (Yes/No)
- Am I prepared for a trial timeline of X months and Y dollars in professional fees? (Yes/No)
