I can help — below is a practical, organized set of questions and a short checklist you can take to your meeting with Robert Kim. It’s focused on deciding whether to accept a settlement or push for trial and on getting ready for the status conference in two months.

Note: this is preparation and general guidance you can use in conversation with your attorney. Your attorney can tell you how it applies to your specific case and Fulton County practice.

1) Big-picture assessment
- How strong is my overall case (factual and legal)? What are the key strengths and weaknesses from your perspective?
- What is the realistic range of outcomes if this goes to trial (e.g., distribution of assets, custody, support)?
- What are the most likely risks and downsides of going to trial in this case?

2) Settlement vs. trial strategy
- Based on the case facts and evidence, do you recommend trying to settle or preparing for trial?
- What would you consider an acceptable settlement for me? (Ask for a numeric/range answer where applicable.)
- What is my Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement (BATNA) — i.e., my fallback if I don’t settle?
- What is your negotiation strategy and typical timeline for settlement talks?
- Are there settlement terms I should absolutely insist on or absolutely avoid?

3) Costs, time, and practical considerations
- What are the estimated attorney fees and other costs (experts, deposition costs, court fees) for settlement negotiations vs. going to trial?
- How long would it likely take to reach trial if we don’t settle (local calendar in Fulton County)?
- Are there temporary orders we should seek now (financial support, custody, use of property) to protect my position while we negotiate or prepare for trial?

4) Evidence and discovery
- What documents and evidence will the court expect for the status conference and for eventual trial?
- What additional discovery do you recommend (bank records, tax returns, appraisals, social media, cellphone records)?
- Which documents should I gather and organize now? (See checklist below.)
- Will we need expert witnesses (financial forensic, valuation, child custody evaluator)? If so, what is the likely cost and timeline to retain them?
- How strong is the documentary evidence from the opposing party? How likely are we to get useful discovery?

5) Witnesses and testimony
- Which witnesses would be critical at trial? Which can be deposed now?
- How would you assess the credibility and expected testimony of each key witness?

6) Settlement terms and details to clarify
- If we settle, what terms should be included to protect me long-term (payments schedule, enforcement provisions, tax allocation, retirement account division, parenting plan specifics, confidentiality, mutual release language)?
- How will post-judgment enforcement work if the respondent violates settlement terms? What remedies are available?
- Should the settlement include a non-disparagement clause or confidentiality provision?
- How should we handle division of retirement accounts, investments, and potential tax consequences?

7) Court procedure & status conference specifics
- What will happen at the Fulton County Civil Division status conference in two months? What should I expect the judge to ask or schedule?
- What do you want prepared or filed before the status conference (proposed orders, disclosures, case plan)?
- Will the status conference likely push us toward mediation or set discovery/trial deadlines?
- Are there local rules or requirements for status conferences in Fulton County we need to follow (form, deadlines, pre-conference filings)?

8) Mediation and settlement mechanics
- Do you recommend mediation or private settlement conference, and when should we do it?
- Who will represent me at mediation — you personally or another attorney in your firm?
- How are mediators in Fulton County typically used in divorce cases? Do judges require mediation before trial?
- If a settlement is reached, what is the process to make it a binding court order?

9) Timing, deadlines, and paperwork
- What deadlines (discovery, disclosures, motions) do I need to be aware of right now and before the status conference?
- What should be submitted to you and by when so you can prepare for the status conference?
- Are there temporary relief motions we should file before the status conference?

10) Communication & decision-making
- How will you keep me informed of settlement offers and developments? What is the best way/time to communicate?
- How much negotiation authority should I give you (e.g., bottom-line numbers or must-have terms)?
- If a settlement offer comes in before we meet, how quickly can we review it and make a decision?

11) Post-trial and appeals
- If we go to trial and lose or partially lose, what are my options for appeal? What are the costs and timelines?
- How enforceable are trial/judgment orders in practice (collection, contempt)?

12) Emotional and contingency considerations
- Are there non-legal factors (stress, schedule, children’s stability) you suggest I weigh heavily before deciding?
- If there is a major change in circumstances (job loss, relocation, new evidence), how will that affect strategy?

Checklist — documents to bring to your meeting / gather now
- Recent pay stubs (3–6 months) and tax returns (last 2–3 years)
- Bank account statements (personal and joint) for last 12–24 months
- Credit card statements
- Mortgage and property deeds, appraisal reports, property tax bills
- Retirement account statements (401(k), IRA) and pension info
- Investment account statements and brokerage records
- Business financials if either party owns a business (profit/loss, balance sheets)
- Proof of debts and liabilities (loans, credit lines)
- Any pre/postnuptial or separation agreements
- Communications relevant to the case (emails, texts) and a timeline of important events
- Child-related documents (school records, custody-related communications)
- Any prior court filings, discovery responses, or orders already issued
- Bills/receipts for major purchases and contributions to marital estate
- List of potential witnesses and short summary of what each would say

Practical next steps I suggest
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim asap to run through the above and ask for his case-specific recommendation.
- Prepare a short written list of your top priorities (financial, custody, timeline, confidentiality) so the lawyer can weigh tradeoffs.
- Ask Robert what he needs you to gather and a timeline for the status conference preparation.

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and requesting a meeting before the status conference.
- Create a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the appointment.
Which would you prefer?

