I can help — below is a focused, practical checklist you can use to prepare for the status conference and to discuss with Robert Kim whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. I’m not a lawyer, so treat this as organizational and strategic guidance you can use when consulting your attorney.

1) Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can tailor this further)
- Are there minor children involved (custody/parenting time/child support)?
- Are there significant marital assets (real estate, business interests, retirement accounts)?
- Do you currently have any temporary orders in place (support, possession of property, exclusive use of home)?
- Has the respondent made a written settlement offer yet?
- Any urgent issues (safety, cash flow, foreclosure, imminent deadlines)?

2) What to bring / documents to gather before the status conference
- Recent pay stubs / proof of income (last 3–6 months)
- Last 2–3 years of tax returns (personal & business if applicable)
- Bank and investment account statements (last 6–12 months)
- Mortgage statements, deeds, car titles, lease agreements
- Retirement account statements (401k, IRA, pension) and valuations
- Credit card and loan statements; debts and liabilities
- Business financials (P&L, balance sheets) if business valuation is an issue
- Appraisals, independent valuations, recent real estate comps
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreement
- Communications or documents relevant to settlement offers or disputed facts
- Evidence/witness lists you may rely on if going to trial (emails, photos, logs)
- Any previous court filings, proposed parenting plans, or proposed settlement terms

3) Questions to ask your attorney at the meeting (grouped by purpose)

A. Case status & immediate procedure
- What exactly will the status conference cover in Fulton County (expected topics/procedure)?
- Are there pre-conference filings I must prepare (status report, disclosures)?
- Which procedural deadlines will be set at the conference?
- What does the judge typically require at the status conference in civil division family cases?

B. Settlement evaluation
- Based on the facts and documents, what is a realistic settlement range for our case?
- What are the key strengths and weaknesses of my position?
- What trade-offs would you recommend (money vs. custody, short-term cash vs. long-term assets)?
- How enforceable are different settlement terms (e.g., lump sum vs. periodic payments)?
- If mediation is recommended, when should we schedule it and who pays for it?

C. Trial readiness & strategy
- How strong is our evidence on the issues that would go to trial?
- What additional discovery, expert reports, or valuations would be required to be trial-ready?
- What witnesses or experts would we need, and what would they cost/time to prepare?
- What is the estimated timeline and cost to proceed to trial in Fulton County?
- What are the realistic outcomes at trial vs. settlement?

D. Costs, timelines, and risk
- Estimated attorney fees and court costs for settlement negotiations vs. preparing for trial.
- Estimated time to resolution if we settle now vs. go to trial.
- Likelihood of appeals or post-judgment disputes and their probable costs.
- Best-case, worst-case, and most-likely outcomes (in plain terms).

E. Practical settlement terms to consider
- Immediate cash/temporary support to address urgent needs
- Division of retirement/vested benefits and tax consequences
- Mortgage/home ownership: buyout vs. sale vs. refinancing
- Child custody, parenting time schedule, decision-making authority
- Child support calculation and duration; spousal support (amount/duration/rehabilitative)
- Health insurance coverage and cost-sharing
- Confidentiality, non-disparagement, and enforcement clauses
- Clean break vs. ongoing payments — pros/cons

F. If you’re leaning to trial: checkpoints and exit points
- What evidence or valuation scorecards would make you reconsider settlement?
- What is a “settlement floor” we will not go below and a “ceiling” we should aim for?
- What interim orders or motions should we file to strengthen our negotiating position?

4) Practical “decision checklist” to evaluate any settlement offer
- Does the offer address my most urgent needs (housing, immediate support)?
- Is the financial valuation fair, considering tax effects and present value?
- Who bears the tax burden of assets being divided?
- Does the settlement give certainty and lower legal costs compared with trial?
- Is the parenting plan workable and enforceable (if applicable)?
- Are there penalties or safeguards if the other side fails to comply?
- Would accepting this settlement close the door to claims I might need later?

5) Steps to take in the next two months (before status conference)
- Within 1–2 weeks: Deliver complete financial documents to your attorney (organized packet).
- Within 2–3 weeks: Lawyer to send initial disclosures or requests for the respondent’s disclosures.
- Weeks 3–6: Consider obtaining valuations or expert reports (real estate, business, forensic accounting) if needed.
- By 4–6 weeks: Discuss mediation timing — set a target date if appropriate.
- 1 week before status conference: Ask attorney for a short pre-conference strategy call (15–30 min) and request a checklist of any filings the court expects.

6) How to talk about settlement vs. trial with Robert Kim (suggested agenda items for your meeting)
- Current status of the case and specific judge handling the conference
- Review of assembled documents and gaps
- Realistic settlement range and key trade-offs
- Cost/time estimates for trial vs. settlement
- Recommended immediate steps (disclosures, mediation, experts)
- Proposed schedule for decision points and mediation
- Any temporary relief or emergency filings to protect your position

7) Offer to help draft communications
- If you want, I can draft:
  - A concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and requesting a preparatory meeting
  - A one-page “status conference packet” checklist you can attach with documents
  - A proposed agenda for mediation or a settlement proposal template

Would you like me to draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and asking for a meeting before the status conference? If so, tell me whether you want it sent now and any tone you prefer (formal/concise/friendly) and I’ll prepare it.

