I can help you get organized and ready to discuss settlement vs. trial with Robert Kim. I’m not a lawyer, but below are focused questions and a document checklist you can take to your attorney — plus a short draft email you can send to request a meeting before the status conference.

Key questions to ask your attorney
- Case merits and risks
  - How strong is our case on the main issues (property division, support, custody, etc.)? What are the biggest weaknesses the respondent could exploit?
  - What is a realistic range of outcomes at trial for each major issue?
  - What facts or evidence would most change your assessment?

- Settlement value and strategy
  - Based on the facts and documents we have, what settlement range would you consider acceptable?
  - What specific terms should be non‑negotiable (or close to it) and which are flexible?
  - Should we aim to resolve now or wait for more discovery/valuation? Why?
  - Would a structured settlement (e.g., periodic payments) make more sense than a lump sum?

- Costs, timeline, and logistics
  - What is the estimated cost (fees, experts, court costs) and timeline to take this to trial?
  - How long is a trial likely to take and when could it realistically happen?
  - What are the chances of appeals or post‑trial enforcement issues?

- Children (if applicable)
  - How do you assess custody/parenting time and child support prospects?
  - Would a settlement provide a more stable outcome for the children than a trial?
  - Are there recommended provisions for schooling, health insurance, extracurricular expenses, or parenting exchanges we should insist on?

- Financial specifics
  - How will retirement accounts, pensions, and tax consequences be treated? Any tax traps to avoid?
  - Do we need forensic accounting or business valuation experts? If so, what is the likely cost and impact?
  - How would debts be allocated under likely settlement vs. trial results?

- Evidence, witnesses, and expert testimony
  - Which documents or witnesses are essential to prove our case at trial?
  - Which expert witnesses might be required (valuation, custody evaluator, forensic accountant)?
  - Are there evidentiary issues (admissibility, hearsay, authentication) we should worry about?

- Court procedure and strategy
  - What local practices or the judge’s preferences in Fulton County might affect strategy?
  - What will happen at the status conference and what should we ask the court to schedule/resolve then?
  - Should we request mediation or court‑ordered settlement conference first?

- Practical decision framework
  - What is our BATNA (best alternative to a negotiated agreement) and worst case? What settlement minimum should I accept?
  - If we accept a settlement, how will enforcement and modifications be handled later?
  - If we decline settlement and lose at trial, what are likely costs and options for appeal?

Document checklist to bring / prepare
- Pleadings and case papers: complaint/petition, answer, temporary orders, discovery responses, motions
- Recent pay stubs, employer income verification
- Last 2–3 years of tax returns (personal and business, if applicable)
- Bank statements (3–12 months), investment account statements
- Mortgage statements, deeds/title documents, vehicle titles
- Credit card statements and loan documents (debts)
- Retirement account statements, pension info, 401(k), IRAs
- Business financials (profit & loss, balance sheets) if you or spouse own a business
- Appraisals/valuations you already have (real estate, business, art, vehicles)
- Insurance documents (health, life, home)
- Receipts or invoices for major expenses, education, medical costs for children
- Communications relevant to the dispute (emails, texts, social media posts)
- Police reports, medical records, or other incident documentation (if relevant)
- Parenting plan proposals, school records, childcare costs (if children involved)
- List of witnesses and brief summary of what each can testify to
- Any prenuptial/postnuptial agreements, previous separation agreements

Preparing for the status conference (2 months out)
- Meet with Robert Kim well before the status conference (ideally 2–4 weeks before) to:
  - Review documents and evidence, confirm any missing items
  - Develop a discovery plan and timeline
  - Decide whether to ask the court for referral to mediation or to schedule interim hearings (temporary support/custody)
  - Prepare a short draft agenda of issues to present at the status conference (what you want the judge to set or order)
- Ask your attorney what they will say at the conference and whether they want you present or available

Suggested short email to Robert Kim (you can edit; I can draft/send it if you want)
