I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for your attorney so you’re ready for the status conference and can make an informed choice about settlement vs. trial. I won’t contact anyone unless you specifically ask me to. Below is a focused checklist and a prioritized set of questions you can ask Robert Kim, plus practical next steps to prepare.

1) Quick clarifying questions for you (so I can tailor this further)
- Any children involved? (custody/visitation/support will change priorities)
- Major assets or a business involved? (real estate, retirement, bank accounts)
- Any temporary orders already in place (custody, support, restraining)?
- Have any settlement offers been made already?
Answering these will let me customize the questions and checklist.

2) Documents to gather and organize (bring copies to your attorney and to the status conference)
- Photo ID and case number / court documents you already have
- Marriage certificate, prenuptial/postnuptial agreements (if any)
- Financial records (last 2–3 years if possible): tax returns, W-2s, 1099s
- Pay stubs, employer benefits, recent bank statements, investment statements
- Retirement account statements, pension info, Social Security estimates
- Mortgage deeds, property titles, vehicle titles, business valuation documents
- Credit card/debt statements and loan documents
- Insurance policies (life, health, homeowners, auto)
- Recent household budget or expense list
- Any communications relevant to the case (texts, emails, social media posts)
- Photos, videos, police reports, medical records, or other evidence
- Documents about children (school records, medical, daycare, extracurricular costs)
- Any prior court orders (temporary orders, custody, support)

3) What to prepare for the status conference (two months out)
- Short written chronology of key events (dates, brief descriptions)
- List of documents you still need and a plan for obtaining them
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses (if any)
- Your settlement bottom line (what you absolutely need) and fallback priorities
- Questions for the judge/attorney about scheduling, discovery deadlines, mediation

4) Core questions to ask Robert Kim — settlement vs. trial (grouped and prioritized)
A. Case strength and likely outcomes
- Based on the facts and documents I’ve provided, how strong is our case on the key issues (custody/support/property)?
- What are the best-case, worst-case, and most-likely outcomes at trial?
- What factual or legal weaknesses should I be aware of?

B. Settlement value and negotiation
- What is a reasonable settlement range for my situation?
- What terms should I insist on (e.g., spousal support, asset division, custody specifics, tax treatment)?
- What concessions would be acceptable to you? What are my walk-away points?
- Can you estimate how enforceable the proposed settlement terms would be?

C. Costs, timing, and emotional toll
- What are the estimated attorney fees and court costs if we go to trial versus settling?
- How long would the process take to get to trial in Fulton County (typical timeline)?
- What is the likely emotional/time burden (depositions, hearings, trial prep)?

D. Risks and benefits of trial
- What are the biggest risks of going to trial in my case?
- What benefits would a trial offer that a settlement wouldn’t (e.g., precedent, full relief)?
- If we lose at trial, what are the appeal possibilities and costs?

E. Procedural and tactical questions
- What discovery remains and how long will it take?
- Are there mandatory mediation or settlement conferences required in Fulton County?
- What should we file or prepare before the status conference (proposed scheduling order, discovery plan, motions)?
- Who will handle witness prep and exhibits? Do we need experts (valuation, custody evaluators)?

F. Settlement mechanics and protections
- If we settle, how will the agreement be documented and enforced?
- Should we include confidentiality, spousal support duration, buyout mechanics, tax language, and clauses about future modifications?
- Can you draft/mediate the settlement terms and explain any tax consequences?

G. Status conference specifics
- What does the judge typically expect at a Fulton County civil status conference?
- What should I be prepared to say or provide at that conference?
- Will we need to request a scheduling order or proposed dates at the conference?

5) Decision framework to discuss with your attorney (how to evaluate accept vs. push)
- Probability of achieving a better result at trial vs. settlement value
- Monetary cost and time to trial vs. settlement timeline
- Emotional cost and privacy (trial is public and often more stressful)
- Enforcement certainty (some settlements offer guaranteed payments; verdicts might be harder to collect)
- Non-monetary priorities (custody time, parenting plan specifics, confidential terms)
- Alternative dispute options (mediation, arbitration, early neutral evaluation)

6) Practical next steps you can take now
- Create a one-page chronology and a separate file with the documents above
- Make a prioritized list of what you need from your lawyer before the status conference
- Ask Robert for a short meeting (phone or in-person) 3–4 weeks before the conference to finalize plans
- Decide your “must-have” terms and your absolute “walk-away” bottom line for settlement
- Keep a secure folder (digital or physical) with copies of everything you may need quickly

7) Offer to help draft communications
- If you want, I can draft a concise email or checklist you can send to Robert Kim asking for a meeting and these specific items/questions to be addressed before the status conference. I can also draft a one-page chronology or a checklist of documents for you to print and take to your attorney.

8) Confidentiality and next steps
- I won’t contact anyone (including any third parties or Vince) without your explicit instruction. If you want me to draft or send an email to Robert or someone else, tell me which and whether to send or save as a draft.

