Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — below is a focused, practical list of questions and a short prep checklist you can use with Robert Kim to weigh settlement vs. trial and to be ready for the status conference in two months.

Quick disclaimer: I’m not a lawyer. Use these items as prompts for discussion with Robert Kim — your attorney should give case-specific legal advice.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case assessment
  - What are the strengths and weaknesses of my case (facts, law, evidence)?
  - How do you assess my likelihood of winning the key issues at trial?
  - Are there legal or evidentiary hurdles that could substantially reduce our chance of success?

- Settlement value & range
  - Based on similar Fulton County cases, what is a realistic settlement range for the main issues (property, support, fees, etc.)?
  - What would you recommend as my opening settlement position and my walk-away/bottom-line (BATNA)?
  - Are there tax or other financial consequences to particular settlement terms I should consider?

- Costs, time, and risk
  - What are the expected attorney fees and other costs (experts, valuation, filing, hearing) for continuing toward trial?
  - How long is a trial likely to take, and how much longer would the overall process be if we go to trial?
  - What are the emotional and practical risks (publicity, stress, disruption) you see in my case?

- Trial readiness & discovery
  - What discovery remains (documents, depositions, interrogatories, expert reports)? What is the timeline to complete it?
  - What evidence or witnesses are critical, and how strong are they?
  - Will we need expert witnesses (valuations, forensics, custody, etc.)? When must they be retained and disclosed?

- Settlement terms and protections
  - What specific settlement terms should we insist on (division of assets, debts, spousal support, child support, custody/visitation if applicable)?
  - How should we draft enforcement, contempt, or modification provisions to make the agreement durable?
  - Should we include confidentiality, non-disparagement, lien releases, or tax allocation language?

- Procedural & strategic questions
  - What will happen at the status conference? Is there anything I must file or bring beforehand?
  - Does the assigned judge have any known preferences or local rules we should account for?
  - Are alternative dispute resolution options available/appropriate (mediation, arbitration)? When should we try them?
  - If settlement talks are productive, should we use a signed term sheet before drafting the final agreement?

- Fees and negotiation tactics
  - Do you recommend negotiating in-house or via formal mediation?
  - Under what conditions would you recommend accepting an offer versus pushing for trial?
  - If we accept a settlement, what is your fee structure for negotiating the agreement and closing it?

- Post-settlement and appeal
  - If we settle, are there any rights I should reserve? If we lose at trial, what are the realistic grounds and timeline for appeal?

Documents to bring / have ready for your meeting
- Up-to-date list of assets and debts (bank accounts, brokerage, retirement, real estate, vehicles)
- Recent bank statements (3–12 months), tax returns (last 2–3 years), pay stubs, and W-2s/1099s
- Mortgage and property deeds, car titles, loan statements
- Credit card and loan statements
- Any prior settlement offers or communications with the respondent
- Contracts, business valuations, partnership agreements (if applicable)
- Insurance policies and beneficiary info
- Records of communications (texts, emails) relevant to the case
- Evidence of expenses, child-related costs (if applicable), and any documentation supporting claims
- Witness names and short descriptions of expected testimony
- Any expert reports you already have

Practical two-month prep plan leading up to status conference
- Week 1: Meet with Robert Kim to get his case assessment and set goals for settlement vs trial; identify immediate discovery needs.
- Week 2–4: Gather and organize financial documents; provide any requested disclosures; list and contact potential witnesses; get initial valuations if needed.
- Week 4–6: Complete targeted discovery (responses, documents); decide whether to retain experts and begin their work.
- Week 6–7: Discuss settlement strategy and a draft settlement proposal/term sheet; consider mediation scheduling.
- Week 7–8 (just before status): Prepare a short status memo for the court (if needed) and confirm what will be presented at the status conference (proposed schedule, discovery timelines, motions).

How to frame the decision to accept settlement vs. go to trial (discussion points)
- Compare expected net recovery (or outcome) under settlement vs. trial after factoring in:
  - Likelihood of success
  - Attorney fees and expert costs
  - Time to resolution and personal cost (stress, lost time)
  - Certainty vs. risk (guaranteed outcome vs. uncertain jury/judge decision)
  - Enforceability and permanence of the settlement terms
- Ask Robert to run a “break-even” analysis: what settlement amount (or specific terms) would make settlement preferable given projected trial costs and risks.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and requesting a meeting (or asking specific items to prepare for the status conference).
- Turn this into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the meeting.

Which would you prefer (drafted email to Robert Kim, checklist, or anything else)? If you want the email, tell me whether to send it now as a draft or to save it to drafts and any preferred tone (formal/informal) or scheduling requests.

