Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and make the most of your status conference and your meeting with Robert Kim. Below are (A) a focused checklist of documents to assemble, (B) specific questions to ask your attorney about whether to accept a settlement or go to trial, (C) a short decision framework to weigh settlement vs trial, and (D) a two-month action plan so you’re ready for the status conference.

A. Documents & evidence checklist (gather these now)
- Identification & case basics
  - Case number, court division (Civil Division, Fulton County), judge’s name, date of status conference.
- Financial documents (last 3–5 years, as available)
  - Pay stubs, W-2s, 1099s, tax returns (personal & business), recent bank statements.
  - Retirement account statements, investment account statements, pension documents.
  - Mortgage statements, deeds, vehicle titles, loan statements, credit card statements, other debts.
  - Business financials if either spouse owns a business (profit/loss, balance sheets).
- Household & expense records
  - Monthly budget, major recurring expenses, receipts showing household spending.
- Supporting documents for claims/position
  - Valuations or appraisals (home, vehicles, business).
  - Records of separate property (inheritances, premarital assets) with proof.
  - Evidence related to income hiding or unusual transfers (if relevant).
- Children (if custody/support is involved)
  - Birth certificates, school records, childcare costs, medical expenses, parenting schedule history, communication logs relevant to custody/time.
- Communications & conduct evidence
  - Relevant emails, texts, social media posts, photos, voicemail records, calendars.
- Prior orders, pleadings, discovery responses
  - Any temporary orders, prior agreements, pleadings, subpoenas, discovery produced so far.
- Witness list & summaries
  - Names, contact info, short summary of each witness’s testimony (if relevant).

B. Questions to ask Robert Kim (structured — copy/paste these into an email or meeting note)
1. Case overview & likely outcomes
  - What is our realistic range of outcomes (best case / worst case / most likely) on the key issues (property division, alimony, child custody/support)?
  - In Fulton County Civil Division, will this be decided by a judge or jury? If judge, do you know this judge’s typical approach in cases like mine?
2. Strength of evidence & needed discovery
  - How strong is our evidence compared to the respondent’s? What are our biggest evidentiary weaknesses?
  - What additional discovery or documentation do we need to strengthen our case? (Specific documents, subpoenas, depositions)
3. Settlement vs trial strategy & risks
  - What are the pros and cons of settling now versus proceeding to trial given our facts?
  - What are the biggest risks and costs (financial, timeline, emotional) if we go to trial?
  - How likely is an appeal if we lose at trial, and how long would that extend resolution?
4. Financial/fees implications
  - Estimate of total legal fees to trial (range) and likely costs for experts/appraisals/depositions.
  - If we settle, what costs would we avoid? If we go to trial, what additional experts would be necessary and how much do they cost?
5. Settlement terms to consider
  - What settlement terms should I consider beyond dollar amounts? (e.g., tax treatment, retirement division, health insurance, debt allocation, confidentiality, parenting schedule, enforcement clauses)
  - Should we include release language, non-disparagement, or structured payments? Pros/cons of lump sum vs periodic payments?
6. Timing & logistics
  - What must be completed before the status conference? What will be on the status conference agenda?
  - How long until we would be trial-ready if we choose that path?
7. Alternative dispute resolution
  - Would mediation or arbitration likely help? When would you recommend attempting mediation?
8. Negotiation posture & authority
  - What settlement range would you advise I offer or accept? What is my “bottom line” or walk-away position?
  - If settlement negotiations start, do you recommend conditioning offers on confidentiality or “without prejudice” terms?
9. Enforcement & modification
  - How easy would it be to enforce a settlement vs an order after trial? How likely would future modification be?
10. Communication & next steps
  - How will you keep me updated on settlement offers? How much time should I expect to have to consider any offer?
  - What do you need from me in the next 2–8 weeks?

C. Decision framework — factors to weigh when considering settlement vs trial
- Certainty vs risk: Settlement gives certainty (even if imperfect); trial is uncertain and could give better or worse outcome.
- Cost/time: Trials cost more and take longer. Consider your tolerance for extended expense and delay.
- Emotional/psychological toll: Trials are stressful; consider emotional costs.
- Non-financial priorities: Privacy, public record, ability to control terms (custody schedules, parenting time) — settlements allow more control.
- Enforcement: Settlements can include clearer enforcement mechanisms (liquidated damages, wage garnishment language).
- Appeal likelihood & finality: Trials produce judgments that may be appealed (extending resolution).
Use a simple matrix: Rate each factor (certainty, cost, privacy, control, enforcement, risk tolerance) for how important it is to you. Share that with your attorney to guide negotiation strategy.

D. Two-month action plan (to be status-conference ready)
Weeks 1–2
- Meet with Robert Kim (or have a phone call) to review current posture and immediate needs.
- Provide all documents from checklist to attorney or a secure folder.
- Clarify settlement bottom line and priorities (money vs time vs custody vs privacy).
Weeks 3–4
- Complete targeted discovery requests your attorney advises (financial disclosures, subpoenas).
- If experts likely needed (valuation, forensic accountant), get quotes and decide whether to retain now.
- Draft possible settlement terms (your ideal and your walk-away).
Weeks 5–6
- Consider mediation scheduling if attorney recommends; get mediator options.
- Prepare witness list and any short witness statement summaries.
- Attorney to propose a discovery schedule and pretrial timeline for status conference.
Week 7–8 (just before status conference)
- Review attorney’s recommended settlement posture and response to potential offers.
- Prepare a one-page case summary for the judge (if appropriate) or for the status conference: issues, procedural posture, discovery needs, estimated trial length, settlement posture.
- Confirm logistics for status conference (who will appear, what documents to file, proposed dates).

E. Practical tips for the status conference
- Bring a concise statement of open issues and what you’re asking (e.g., “Need 60 days to complete forensic accounting; prefer mediation by X date; estimated trial 3 days”).
- Ask the judge (through counsel) to set clear deadlines for discovery and motion practice.
- If settlement is possible, ask the court to set a timeline for mediation or a referral to ADR.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft an email or a one-page agenda/one-sheet to send to Robert Kim with the questions above.
- Help you create the “bottom line” decision matrix (we can list dollar ranges, custody priorities, and red lines).

Do you want me to draft a ready-to-send email to Robert Kim with the key questions and the summary of what you’ll bring to the status conference?

