I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for your attorney so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. Below is a practical, prioritized plan: documents to gather, focused questions to ask Robert Kim, a decision checklist you can use to weigh settlement vs trial, and a suggested timeline of tasks before the status conference in two months. Tell me if you want this turned into an email or one-page agenda for your meeting with Robert.

1) Top documents to bring to your attorney (gather these now)
- Full case file (pleadings, motions, orders, docket entries)
- Any settlement offers (written or email summaries)
- Financial documents:
  - Last 3–5 years of tax returns
  - Recent pay stubs, employment verification
  - Bank statements (last 12 months)
  - Retirement account statements, pension docs
  - Brokerage account statements
  - Mortgage deeds, property deeds, vehicle titles
  - Credit card statements, loan statements, other debts
  - Business financials if either party owns a business
- Monthly budget / household expenses, child care costs
- Documents relevant to custody/parenting (school records, medical, calendars of parenting time)
- Communications: text messages, emails, social media posts relevant to the dispute
- Any police reports, protective orders, medical records (if applicable)
- Appraisals, property valuations, expert reports
- Witness contact list and summaries of what each saw or knows

2) Essential questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped by topic)
A. Case strength & likely outcomes
- Based on the evidence now, what is the realistic range of possible outcomes (best case, worst case, most likely) for the main issues (property division, support, custody)?
- How strong is our evidence on each major issue? Where are the weaknesses?
- What legal standards will the judge apply in the civil division in Fulton County for the issues in my case?

B. Risks, costs, and timing
- What are the estimated attorney fees and court costs to take this to trial (and to a final judgment)? Include likely costs of experts, depositions, investigators.
- How long will a trial likely take (trial length and total timeline to final judgment)?
- What are the risks of an adverse ruling? What are likely post-trial or appeal options and timelines?

C. Settlement specifics to evaluate
- If I entertain settlement, what minimum terms should I accept to meet my goals? (ask attorney to give a recommended “floor” and “target”)
- For financial settlements, what exact items should be included (alimony/maintenance, child support, division of assets/debts, tax treatment, QDROs for retirement)?
- For custody/parenting agreements: what precise schedule, decision-making language, dispute-resolution procedures, and modification rules should be included?
- Do you recommend including confidentiality provisions or a release? What are the pros/cons?
- How will enforcement work (contempt remedies, wage garnishment, liens)?

D. Evidence & discovery
- What additional discovery do we need (depositions, document requests, subpoenas, forensic accounting, experts)?
- Are there immediate steps to preserve or obtain key evidence?
- Which witnesses are critical, and what would they testify to? Are expert witnesses likely necessary?

E. Settlement negotiation strategy
- What settlement strategy do you recommend (mediate, negotiation exchange, structured settlement)?
- Would mediation be useful? If so, when and who should mediate?
- What concessions are reasonable to give, and which are non-negotiable?
- If we proceed to mediation or settlement talks, what are typical timelines and confidentiality protections?

F. Trial preparation & logistics
- What pretrial motions should we consider (summary judgment, exclusions, protective orders)?
- What is needed for the status conference in two months? (proposed schedule, discovery plan, pretrial deadlines)
- What do you want me to prepare/bring to the status conference (proposed orders, witness lists, exhibits, settlement posture)?
- What is the likely courtroom process in Fulton County for a family/divorce trial? (bench trial vs. jury if relevant)

G. Practical & personal considerations
- What monetary and non-monetary tradeoffs should I consider (speed, certainty, privacy, emotional cost, relationship with respondent)?
- If I accept a settlement, how permanent is it? How difficult is it to modify later if circumstances change?
- How enforceable is any financial award given the respondent’s ability to pay?

3) A concise decision checklist (use this to score a settlement offer)
For any settlement offer, ask Robert to help you fill this in and set thresholds (e.g., minimum acceptable settlement).
- Financial difference: Settlement amount vs. expected trial award (net after costs)
- Certainty: How certain is the trial outcome? (high/moderate/low)
- Cost & time: Estimated extra cost and months/years to finality
- Emotional/relationship cost: How much stress/disruption will trial impose?
- Confidentiality/privacy: Is confidentiality important? (yes/no)
- Enforceability: Likelihood you can collect/enforce the award
- Children/family stability (if applicable): Does settlement better protect children’s interests?
If the settlement’s net benefit (after costs, risk, and emotional toll) exceeds your threshold, consider accepting; if not, prepare to litigate.

4) Practical items to prepare before the status conference (two-month timeline)
- Week 0–1: Gather documents above and give them to Robert. Ask for an initial assessment memo of case strengths/weaknesses.
- Week 2–3: Serve or respond to any outstanding discovery; identify experts and draft witness list.
- Week 4: Discuss settlement posture and bottom line; decide whether to request mediation before the conference.
- Week 5–6: Prepare proposed scheduling order and list of pending motions for status conference. Prepare exhibit list and timeline of major events.
- Week 7: Mock run-through with Robert of what will be said at status conference; finalize any filings.
- Status conference: Attend with Robert, be ready to state discovery timelines, mediation plans, and proposed trial dates.

5) Example phrasing for questions during your meeting with Robert
- “Given our evidence, how would you rate our chances on (issue) — high/moderate/low — and why?”
- “What exact settlement number or terms would you advise as my minimum and my realistic target?”
- “What are the total projected costs to try this case, including experts, and how do they affect a settlement decision?”
- “What do you recommend I say (or not say) during settlement talks or mediation?”
- “If we go to trial and win, how long until I actually receive the award and how enforceable is it?”

6) Other things to confirm with Robert
- Ask for a written estimate of fees and an updated budget for trial vs. settlement.
- Confirm communication plan and how/when he will update you on offers.
- Ask whether he recommends any immediate protective steps (temporary orders, asset preservation).
- Ask about alternative dispute resolution clauses and what confidentiality provisions he thinks appropriate.

7) Offer to help now
- I can draft a short email / meeting agenda to Robert Kim listing these documents and the top questions you want answered.
- I can create a one-page decision worksheet you can take to your meeting to score settlement offers.
Which would you like me to prepare for you now?

