I can help — this is a good time to get a clear checklist and a set of focused questions to ask Robert Kim so you can decide whether to accept a settlement or prepare for trial. Below are (A) the key issues to clarify with your attorney, (B) a checklist of documents to gather before the status conference, (C) practical next steps for the two‑month status conference, and (D) a short email template you can send to Robert Kim. Tell me if you want me to draft that email for you (saved as a draft or sent now).

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case strength & realistic outcomes
  - What do you view as the strongest facts/evidence in my favor? The weakest?
  - Based on your experience and similar cases in Fulton County, what are realistic settlement ranges/outcomes for the main issues (division of assets, spousal support, child custody/support if relevant)?
  - What are the biggest risks of going to trial (e.g., unpredictable rulings, costs, time)?

- Settlement vs. trial strategy
  - Given the facts and evidence, do you recommend negotiating a settlement or pushing toward trial? Why?
  - What is my Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement (BATNA)? What is the worst likely outcome?
  - How much negotiation authority should I give you (e.g., dollar thresholds, non‑monetary tradeoffs)? Do I need to be present for settlement talks?
  - If we settle, what essential protections should be written into the agreement (enforceability, tax treatment, confidentiality, release language)?

- Costs, time, and process
  - What are estimated attorney fees and court costs through a settlement vs through trial (best, expected, worst-case)?
  - What is the likely timeline for mediation, discovery completion, pretrial motions, and trial in Fulton County?
  - Are there mandatory mediation or settlement conferences in this division before trial?

- Evidence & discovery
  - What evidence or documentation would materially change your assessment?
  - What discovery will we need (financial disclosures, depositions, subpoenas)? Any anticipated disputes about discovery?
  - Do we need forensic accountants, valuation experts, or private investigators? When should they be engaged?

- Children / Custody / Support (if applicable)
  - If children are involved: what custody/visitation outcomes are likely? What evidence matters most (parenting schedules, school records, communications, allegations)?
  - Should we request temporary custody/support orders now?

- Specifics about settlement terms
  - If settlement is reached, should payments be lump-sum or structured? Are there enforcement mechanisms we should include (liens, wage garnishment, life insurance for support)?
  - How do we address retirement accounts, tax consequences, ownership transfers, title changes?
  - Should there be non-disparagement, confidentiality, or mutual release clauses?

- Court logistics & local practice
  - Are there Fulton County / Georgia family law or civil division local rules/forms we need to file before the status conference?
  - What should I expect at the status conference? What will the judge likely ask or require?
  - Are there common procedural motions we should prepare (temporary relief, injunctions, discovery motions)?

- Settlement mechanics & protections
  - Who prepares the settlement agreement and who reviews it? How long should I expect to review before signing?
  - Can we include penalty, escrow, or secured payment provisions if the respondent agrees to installment payments?
  - How will enforcement work if the opposing party breaches the settlement?

- Next steps & responsibilities
  - What do you need from me immediately (documents, dates, witness names)?
  - What are the deadlines between now and the status conference? What will you do vs. what I must do?
  - How often will we communicate and how will you keep me updated on settlement offers?

B. Documents to gather (high priority first)
- Identification of parties and case paperwork: Complaint/Petition, Answer, current pleadings, case number, judge name, notices from court.
- Financial documents (last 3–5 years if possible): tax returns, W-2s/1099s, pay stubs, bank statements, brokerage and investment account statements, retirement account statements (401k, IRA), mortgage statements, deeds/titles, vehicle registrations, credit card statements, business financials if self-employed.
- Proofs of ownership and valuations: appraisals, recent sale offers, valuations for business interests.
- Debts and liabilities: loan agreements, promissory notes, outstanding balances.
- Communications and records: emails, texts, social media messages relevant to the issues, calendars showing custody/parenting time, photos/videos if relevant.
- Child-related documents (if applicable): school records, medical records, childcare expenses, extracurricular billing, evidence of primary caregiving, visitation schedules.
- Evidence of misconduct (if relevant and admissible): police reports, restraining orders, financial misconduct documents, proof of hidden assets.
- Prior settlement offers, correspondence with opposing counsel, and mediation notes.
- Any existing temporary orders, child support calculations, or enforcement actions.

C. Preparations for the status conference (2 months out)
- Ask your attorney to prepare a short case summary/status memo for the court: issues, discovery status, outstanding motions, proposed schedule.
- Confirm deadlines: initial disclosures, expert disclosures, discovery cutoff, mediation date proposals, pretrial conference date.
- Ask whether the court expects a proposed scheduling order. If so, get your attorney’s proposed timeline.
- Discuss whether to request mediation before the status conference or have a settlement offer ready to present.
- Start gathering and organizing documents now (use a labelled folder or a shared secure folder for the attorney).
- Prepare a short personal statement of priorities (what you must have in a settlement vs what you can compromise on).
- Prepare a witness list and short summary of what each would testify to.

D. How to evaluate an offered settlement
- Quantify: compare financial terms (present value) and add cost/time savings of avoiding trial.
- Non‑financial terms: custody arrangements, confidentiality, future control of property, restraining provisions, public record vs private.
- Risk tolerance: likelihood of winning at trial, chance of worse outcome, emotional and time costs.
- Enforceability: is the settlement secured (lien, escrow) or at risk if the other party defaults?
- Tax and long-term consequences: one-time lump sum may have different tax implications; check with attorney/accountant.

E. Sample email you can send to Robert Kim
(You can copy/paste and adjust tone or details.)

Subject: Preparation & Questions Before Status Conference — [Case Name/Number]

Hi Robert,

Our status conference is scheduled in about two months. I want to be prepared and would appreciate your guidance on whether we should pursue settlement or prepare for trial.

Attached are the documents I have so far. Can we discuss the following at our next meeting or call?
- Your assessment of the strengths/weaknesses of my case and realistic settlement ranges for the main issues.
- Estimated costs and timeline for settlement vs trial (including likely court dates, discovery deadlines, and mediation).
- What discovery or evidence would most improve our position, and whether we should retain any experts (accountant/valuer).
- What specific settlement terms and protections you recommend (payment structure, enforcement, confidentiality, tax considerations).
- Your recommendation on negotiation authority I should give you and whether I should attend settlement talks/mediation.
- What you/ I need to prepare before the status conference (documents, witness list, proposed schedule).
- Any Fulton County or Georgia family/civil rules or forms we need now.

Also: please identify the top 3 to 5 items I should prioritize gathering in the next two weeks.

Thank you — please let me know when you have availability for a 30‑minute call.

Best,
[Your name]

F. Final practical tips
- Start organizing documents now in a single secure folder and label them clearly by topic/date.
- Keep an exact copy of any settlement offers (don’t sign anything until your attorney reviews it).
- Ask your attorney to put evaluation scenarios in writing (best/expected/worst) so you can compare.
- If emotions are high, try to decide your core objectives ahead of negotiation (money, custody, timeline, privacy).

Would you like me to:
- Draft that email and save it to your drafts or send it to Robert Kim at kimassociates@example.com (please confirm his email address), or
