Yes — I can help. Below I’ve put together (1) a focused list of questions to ask Robert Kim about settlement vs. trial, (2) a document checklist to gather and bring to your meeting/status conference, (3) decision factors to weigh when choosing settlement or trial, and (4) a short prep timeline for the next two months. If you’d like, I can also draft an email or message to send to Robert Kim with these questions and attach your priorities.

1) Top questions to ask your attorney (prioritized)
- What is your honest assessment of the strengths and weaknesses of my case? What facts or evidence most help my position, and what harms it?
- Based on your experience in Fulton County (civil division) and with this judge, what are realistic outcomes at trial and what settlement ranges are typical?
- How likely are we to obtain my desired relief at trial (percent confidence and reasons)?
- What is the estimated cost (attorney fees, court costs, experts) and time (length of trial, overall timeline) if we go to trial?
- If we settle, what key terms should I insist on (e.g., lump sum vs. installments, specific asset transfers, who pays what debts, spousal support duration/amount, custody/visitation details)?
- How would the settlement be enforced and what protections (e.g., judgment, lien, security interest, escrow, QDRO for retirement accounts) will be included?
- Are there tax consequences for proposed settlement options I should consider?
- What are my non-negotiables and fallback points in a settlement — help me define a minimum acceptable settlement and a walk-away point.
- What discovery remains? Will additional documentation or depositions materially change our position?
- Do we need expert witnesses (valuations, forensics, custody experts)? If so, who and when would they be retained, and what do they cost?
- Is mediation or other ADR likely required or advisable before the status conference or trial? Can you represent me in mediation?
- What motions or pretrial filings should we prepare before the status conference (financial affidavits, temporary orders, discovery motions, pretrial statements)?
- Are there temporary reliefs we should ask for now (temporary support, exclusive use of home, temporary custody or restraining orders)?
- What is the judge’s typical approach in these matters (pace, preference for settlements, strictness on discovery/local rules)?
- If we accept a settlement, what language is needed to avoid loopholes (clear release language, confidentiality, enforcement remedies, future modification rules)?
- If we go to trial and lose, what are our appeal options and timelines? What are the costs/risks of an appeal?
- How will you communicate settlement offers, and do I authorize you to negotiate within set limits (if I give settlement authority, specify parameters)?
- What immediate steps should I take in the next 2–4 weeks to strengthen my position?

2) Document checklist — what to assemble and bring to the attorney meeting and to the status conference
Financial / property documents
- Recent pay stubs (last 3 months) and employment verification
- Last 3 years tax returns (all schedules) and W-2/1099s
- Recent bank statements (all accounts — last 12–24 months)
- Investment/retirement account statements (last statements)
- Mortgage/loan statements, deeds, titles for real property and vehicles
- Credit card statements, lines of credit, outstanding debts
- Business financials if either spouse owns a business: profit/loss, balance sheets, tax returns, contracts
- Appraisals, valuations, or recent offers related to major assets

Custody/children (if applicable)
- Childcare/education expenses, schedules, IEPs, school records
- Records of parenting time, calendar/log of interactions, communication logs
- Any records of abuse, CPS involvement, police reports, medical records

Communications & evidence
- Relevant emails, texts, photos, social media posts (organized by date and context)
- Important contracts, leases, pre-/post-nuptial agreements
- Witness names and short statement of what they can testify to (dates/contact info)

Other
- Medical records relevant to claims
- Financial affidavit templates or court-required forms (ask Robert which forms Fulton County needs)
- Any prior settlement offers, court orders, pleadings filed so far

3) Key legal and practical factors to weigh before deciding settlement vs. trial
- Certainty vs. risk: Settlement = known outcome; trial = uncertain, time-consuming, costly.
- Time and money: Trials are expensive and can take months to years (trial prep, discovery, experts).
- Emotional cost and privacy: Trials are public and stressful; settlements can be confidential.
- Enforcement: Even a favorable judgment must be enforced — settlement terms can include immediate enforcement mechanisms.
- Business/asset valuation complexity: If marital assets are hard to value (businesses, pensions), you may need experts; settlement can avoid valuation battles.
- Child welfare: Courts prioritize best interests; if custody is central, consider how a trial could affect relationships and exposure.
- Precedent and appeals: Trial outcomes carry legal finality but appeals are limited and expensive.
- Time-sensitivity: If you need immediate relief (support, exclusive use of home), settlement may provide faster solutions via temporary orders.

4) Practical next steps and a 2-month prep timeline before the status conference
Week 0 (now)
- Schedule meeting with Robert Kim soon. Bring prioritized questions and the assembled documents above.
- Write down your goals and rank them (top priorities, acceptable compromises, absolute dealbreakers).
- Decide whether you will give Robert limited settlement authority (and the exact numeric/range limits).

Weeks 1–3
- Produce and exchange initial discovery documents as advised by counsel (financial affidavit, bank statements, tax returns).
- Identify and contact potential expert witnesses if needed (valuation, forensic accountant, custody expert).
- Organize evidence chronologically and create a short facts timeline (one page) for counsel.

Weeks 4–6
- Consider mediation — ask counsel to schedule or prepare for mediation if recommended.
- Prepare witness list and short summary of their expected testimony.
- Draft proposed settlement terms and a “bottom line” written for counsel to use in negotiations.

Week 7–8 (just before status conference)
- Review and finalize temporary motions or filings counsel wants to submit.
- Meet with counsel to rehearse the status conference: what you will say, what you authorize counsel to propose/agree to.
- Confirm what you should do at the conference (e.g., whether you should attend in person).

5) Questions about the status conference itself (ask Robert)
- What does the court expect at a status conference in Fulton County for a divorce/civil family matter?
- Will the judge want a proposed schedule (discovery deadlines, expert disclosures, trial date)?
- Is settlement/motion scheduling or mediation likely to be ordered at the status conference?
- Do I need to file any forms or financial disclosures in advance of the conference?

6) How I can help now
- I can draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and asking for a meeting or written answers.
- I can put these questions into a printable checklist or a one-page meeting agenda so you can take it to your appointment.
- I can help you draft your “bottom line” settlement authority statement (range, non-negotiables) to give to counsel.

Would you like me to:
