Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and ready for the status conference and the decision whether to accept a settlement or push to trial. Below I’ve put together:

- a prioritized checklist of immediate steps before the status conference (in two months),
- a detailed list of documents to gather,
- a structured set of questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic),
- a practical decision framework you can use to evaluate any settlement offer,
- and suggested next-step actions (including an offer to draft an email to your lawyer asking these questions or scheduling a meeting).

Tell me if you want me to draft a message to Robert Kim (Kim & Associates) using these items — I can create a polished email or save it as a draft.

Immediate priorities (next few weeks)
- Schedule a meeting with Robert Kim well before the status conference so you have time to prepare.
- Gather and organize all financial and custody-related documents (see list below).
- Ask your attorney whether any immediate temporary orders (support, custody, protection) need to be requested before or at the status conference.
- Confirm the court’s required filings and deadlines for Fulton County (civil/court rules, financial affidavit deadlines, local scheduling orders).
- Discuss whether mediation or settlement discussions are required or recommended before the status conference.

Documents to gather and organize
Financial documents (last 2–3 years, more if available)
- Tax returns (personal and business) — last 2–3 years
- W-2s, 1099s, K-1s
- Recent pay stubs and proof of income (last 3 months)
- Bank statements (all accounts) — last 12–24 months
- Retirement account statements (401(k), IRAs, pensions) and account history
- Brokerage statements and investment valuations
- Mortgage statements, deeds, titles to real property
- Car titles and loan statements
- Business financials (profit/loss, balance sheet, valuation reports) if either party owns a business
- Credit card statements and records of debts/loans
- Records of large gifts/transfers, inheritances, or separate property claims

Support, expenses & budgets
- Household budget/expense records
- Child-related expenses (daycare, school, medical, activities)
- Healthcare and insurance documents (premiums, coverage)
- Any alimony/child support history or prior agreements

Communications & evidence
- Relevant emails, texts, social media posts, photographs, recordings (preserve originals)
- Documents showing contributions to property or debts paid
- Any restraining orders, police reports or court documents

Parenting / custody (if applicable)
- School records, schedules, extracurricular calendars
- Records of parenting time and any concerning incidents
- Any third-party evaluations, counselor reports, or prior custody assessments

Other legal documents
- Existing separation or temporary orders
- Prenuptial or postnuptial agreements (if any)
- Prior settlement offers or correspondence

Questions to ask your attorney
I. Case evaluation & likely outcome
- Based on the facts and documentation we have, how strong are our main claims/defenses (property division, custody, support, etc.)?
- How would you summarize the case’s key strengths and weaknesses?
- What outcome do you think a judge is likely to order at trial on the main issues? (Ask for ranges, not just yes/no.)
- How have similar cases in Fulton County been decided (especially by the judge handling our case)?

II. Settlement strategy & terms
- Do you recommend opening settlement negotiations now, or waiting? Why?
- What settlement range would you consider reasonable given our likely trial outcome? What would you consider a “good” versus a “bad” offer?
- What specific settlement terms matter most to protect my interests (e.g., lump sum vs periodic payments, QDRO for retirement, custody schedule, parenting time, health insurance, tax allocation)?
- Should we require security for payments (e.g., lump-sum, lien, insurance, escrow)?
- Should confidentiality be part of any settlement? Pros/cons in our situation?
- What concessions might we be willing to make, and what are absolute deal-breakers?

III. Costs, timing & risks
- What are the estimated attorney fees and court costs to get to trial (range)?
- How long will trial preparation and the trial itself likely take?
- What are the financial and non-financial risks of going to trial (costs, emotional/time burden, public record)?
- If we go to trial and win, how long might it take to receive enforcement or payments? Are appeals likely?

IV. Discovery & evidence
- What additional discovery/documentation do you recommend to strengthen our position?
- What witnesses or expert reports (valuation, custody evaluator, forensic accountant) will likely be necessary or helpful?
- Are there evidentiary problems we should anticipate (admissibility, hearsay, authentication)?

V. Custody / child-focused questions (if applicable)
- What custody arrangement do you recommend as our goal and why?
- Would a custody evaluation or parenting coordinator help our case?
- How does the judge typically decide custody matters in Fulton County (what factors are emphasized)?

VI. Settlement mechanics & post-settlement issues
- If we settle, how should we structure spousal support/child support to protect me (tax impact, modification terms, duration)?
- How do we handle retirement account division (QDROs) and tax consequences?
- What provisions should we include for enforcement, default, or future modifications?
- If the other side breaches a settlement, what remedies (liquidated damages, contempt) should we include?

VII. Court process & status conference specifics
- What will happen at the status conference? What should I expect to be asked or required to present?
- What filings does the court expect before the status conference (docketing statements, disclosures, proposed scheduling order)?
- Are settlement conferences or mediation deadlines set by the court in this case?
- Do we need temporary orders set before trial (support, exclusive use of home, custody schedule)?

Practical decision framework for settlement vs trial
- Estimate likely trial award (best estimate and reasonable range) and subtract estimated costs (attorney fees, expert fees, court costs) to calculate your expected net from trial.
- Compare the trial net to the settlement offer net (consider present value if payments are periodic).
- Factor in non-financial considerations: time, emotional cost, publicity (trials are public), impact on children, certainty vs uncertainty.
- Consider enforcement and security: an assured but slightly lower secure settlement may be better than an uncertain larger trial award that’s hard to collect.
- Ask: “Would I be content with this result if I could not appeal and if the terms were final?” If yes, settlement may be appropriate.
- Consult your attorney’s recommended probability of success at trial and let that probability weigh into the expected-value math.

Practical negotiation points to consider
- Break settlement into components: financial, custody, tax, confidentiality, enforcement.
- Insist on clarity and mechanisms for enforcement (liens, escrow, QDRO).
- Consider staged settlement offers (e.g., initial counteroffers, mediation).
- Preserve options to settle short of trial while still preparing for trial — leverage is often improved by good preparation.

What to prepare for the status conference
- A clear summary of what you want (goals) and absolute deal-breakers.
- A short chronology of important events and evidence highlights.
- A list of outstanding discovery items and witnesses.
- Proposed scheduling plan (discovery deadlines, expert disclosure dates, mediation deadline, estimated trial length).
- A note on whether you are open to mediation and by what date.

Suggested next steps for you now
1. Meet with Robert Kim ASAP and go through the above questions — request his assessment and a written estimate of costs and a proposed timeline.
2. Ask him to run a simple expected-value analysis (trial probability × expected award − expected costs) and compare to any settlement offers.
3. Decide whether to commence intensive discovery and expert work now (to strengthen settlement leverage).
4. Set a mediation/deadline strategy leading up to the status conference (e.g., attempt mediated settlement X weeks before the conference).
5. If you want, I can draft an email to Robert Kim summarizing these points and requesting a meeting and the specific written answers you want before the status conference.

Would you like me to draft that email to Robert Kim (Kim & Associates) for you? If so, tell me:
- whether you want to request a phone call or an in-person meeting,
- any immediate deadlines you have,
- whether you want to attach any documents now, and
- whether to include a short chronology of facts for his review.

If you prefer, I can also format this into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your attorney meeting. Which would be most helpful?

