I can help you get ready. Below is a practical, organized set of questions to take to Robert Kim, the documents and evidence to bring, what to expect at the civil-division status conference in two months, the main factors to weigh when choosing settlement vs. trial, and suggested next steps. If you want, I can also draft an email to your attorney or produce a printable checklist for the meeting.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (use these to structure the meeting)
- Case assessment and odds
  - Based on the facts and documents we have, what is your realistic assessment of the likely outcome on the main issues (property division, spousal support, child custody/support if applicable)?
  - What are the strongest and weakest parts of our case?
- Settlement vs. trial strategy
  - What would a fair settlement look like, in your view? What is a reasonable settlement range (best case / likely / minimum acceptable)?
  - What are the concrete legal and practical advantages of settling now versus pushing for trial?
  - What are the main risks and downsides of settling (e.g., giving up future claims, enforcement problems, tax consequences)?
  - If we go to trial, what are the biggest risks (cost, time, unpredictable judge/jury outcomes)?
- Costs and timeline
  - Estimated attorney fees and court costs if we settle now vs. if we proceed to trial (include experts, discovery, depositions).
  - Timeline for discovery, motions, the pretrial schedule, and trial (and whether appeals are likely/possible).
  - What will happen at the status conference in two months and what deadlines will be set?
- Evidence and discovery
  - What additional documents or evidence do we need to strengthen the case?
  - Who would we need to depose or call as witnesses? Will we need expert witnesses (valuation, forensic accountant, custody evaluator)?
  - What is the discovery plan and approximate cost/time for obtaining financial records, business valuations, etc.?
- Temporary/interim issues
  - Should we seek or modify any temporary orders (support, possession, injunctions) before the status conference or immediately after?
- Settlement mechanics and enforceability
  - If we reach a settlement, how should it be structured (lump sum, periodic payments, property transfer, retirement asset division)?
  - How enforceable is the proposed settlement? What terms should be included to protect me (attorney-fee provisions, enforcement remedies, tax allocation)?
  - Are confidentiality or non-disparagement clauses recommended and how do they affect enforceability?
- Children and support (if applicable)
  - How will custody and parenting time be determined in a settlement vs. a court decision?
  - How is child support calculated in Georgia and how would a settlement compare to guideline support?
  - What about relocation, schooling, medical decisions — what protective terms should be included?
- Tax and financial consequences
  - What are the tax implications (capital gains, alimony, division of retirement accounts)? Should we consult a tax advisor?
- Negotiation process
  - Do you recommend mediation, neutral evaluation, or collaborative law before trial? When should we attempt mediation?
  - Under what conditions should I accept a settlement? What is our “walk-away” point (BATNA)?
- Communication and decisions
  - How will you keep me informed about settlement offers? How much time will I get to consider an offer before you accept or reject?
  - If I disagree with your recommendation, how do we handle final decision-making?
- Next steps for me
  - What should I be doing now (gathering documents, limiting communications, preserving evidence)?

B. Documents and evidence to gather now
- Financial
  - Recent pay stubs, last 2–3 years of W-2s and tax returns (personal and business), 1099s
  - Bank statements (personal and business) for the last 2–3 years
  - Credit card statements and loan/mortgage statements
  - Retirement account statements (401(k), IRAs, pensions), brokerage accounts
  - Deeds, titles, vehicle registrations, mortgage documents
  - Business ownership documents, profit/loss statements, business tax returns
  - Valuations you already have for property/assets
- Expenses and lifestyle
  - Monthly household budget, major recurring expenses, childcare or school costs
- Children (if applicable)
  - School records, activities, medical records, childcare costs
- Communications and timeline
  - Relevant emails, texts, social media posts, and a timeline of major events (dates of separation, incidents, offers)
- Legal documents
  - Any pleadings already filed, temporary orders, prior settlement offers, discovery responses, subpoenas
- Evidence of misconduct (if relevant)
  - Documentation supporting claims of waste, dissipation of assets, abuse, or neglect
Bring organized copies (and a secure digital folder) so your attorney can review quickly.

C. What to expect at the status conference (civil division, Fulton County)
- The judge typically wants to set a scheduling order: deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation, expert reports, and a trial date window.
- The court may ask whether parties are open to mediation or other ADR and whether any interim (temporary) relief is needed.
- If there are pressing temporary issues (support, exclusive use of home, restraining orders), the judge may schedule separate hearings.
- Come prepared with proposed deadlines and any pending motions or discovery disputes to raise with the court.

D. Factors to weigh when deciding settlement versus trial
- Probability of success: attorney’s assessment of win/loss and which issues are most predictable.
- Financial cost: fees for discovery, experts, trial vs. the certainty of a settlement.
- Time and stress: how long you’re willing to be in litigation and the emotional toll.
- Privacy: settlements are private; trials are public records.
- Enforceability: do you trust the other party to follow a settlement? Is there a way to secure payments (liens, wage garnishment, escrow)?
- Children’s welfare: stability and predictability for kids vs. contested resolution.
- Tax consequences and asset division complexities.
- Precedent/risk: a court ruling could create an outcome you cannot unwind; a settlement can be tailored.

E. Practical checklist to bring to the meeting with Robert Kim
- Concise summary of what you want (top objectives and non-negotiables)
- Prioritized list of issues (e.g., keep the house, custody time, child support amount)
- Documents from section B, organized and labeled
- Any offers already made or received in writing
- Questions from section A (printed or on your phone)
- A short list of acceptable settlement terms and your absolute dealbreakers

F. Suggested phrasing for two crucial questions in the meeting
- “Given everything we have now, what would you recommend: accept a negotiated settlement within X range or prepare for trial? Why?”
- “If we plan to trial, what specific evidence and experts must we have ready before we can realistically expect to win on the major issues?”

G. Next steps I can help with right now
- Draft a concise email to Robert Kim listing these questions and requesting a status conference preparation meeting.
- Produce a printable one-page checklist you can bring to the status conference.
- Help you prioritize documents and organize them into a shareable folder.

Privacy note: I will not share or forward any of your information to third parties unless you explicitly ask me to. (If you want me to draft a message to your attorney or create a secure checklist, tell me who it should be sent to and whether to save as a draft or send.)

