I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you prepare—here’s a focused set of things to ask Robert Kim and a checklist of documents and prep so you can decide whether to settle or push for trial and be ready for the status conference.

Top-level approach
- Before you meet, clarify your priorities (e.g., money, timing, privacy, custody, avoiding drawn-out litigation). Know your Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement (BATNA) and Worst Alternative (WATNA) — what minimum outcome you’d accept and what you want ideally.
- Ask your attorney for a clear recommendation based on those goals and the case facts (noting strengths/weaknesses, costs, and timelines).

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
(You can copy these into an email or bring them to your meeting.)

Big-picture / strategy
- Based on our facts and evidence, what are the realistic outcome ranges (best, most likely, worst)?
- Given my goals, do you recommend we accept a settlement or proceed to trial? Why?
- What are the strongest and weakest parts of our case?
- What are the likely key legal issues the court will decide?

Settlement-specific
- What would be a fair settlement range for the issues in this case (property division, support, custody if applicable)?
- What is the minimum settlement I should accept (your recommended reservation value)?
- What exact terms should be included in any settlement agreement to protect me (payments, security, timing, tax treatment, release of claims, confidentiality, non-disparagement, enforcement, modification)?
- How should settlement payments be structured (lump sum vs installments)? Should we require a lien, bond, or other security?
- If retirement accounts or pensions are involved, will we need a QDRO or other instruments? Who prepares it and when?
- Are there tax consequences to any proposed settlement terms (capital gains, alimony taxable/ deductible, etc.) — should I consult a CPA?

Trial readiness, risks & timeline
- If we go to trial, what is the likely timeline until trial and how long will trial take?
- What is the likelihood of success at trial in your professional judgment?
- What are the material risks if we lose at trial (financial, custody, enforcement, appeals)?
- If we go to trial and win, what are the practical steps to enforce the judgment?

Costs & resources
- What are the estimated attorney fees and court costs to bring this to trial vs reasonable settlement negotiations? How much more could trial cost?
- Are there cost-saving alternatives (mediation, arbitration, limited scope motions) you recommend?
- How will fee arrangements and billing be handled if this becomes contested?

Evidence & discovery
- What additional documents or evidence will the court likely want to see?
- Do we need expert witnesses (valuation, forensic accountant, custody evaluator, vocational expert)? What do they cost and how much will they help?
- What discovery should we open now (interrogatories, document requests, subpoenas, depositions)? What’s the plan/timeline for discovery?

Status conference — what to expect and how to prepare
- What will happen at the status conference in two months? (e.g., scheduling order, discovery deadlines, ADR requirement)
- What should I bring or file before the status conference (proposed schedule, list of issues, disclosures)?
- Will the judge expect a settlement posture or mediation before the conference?

Practical & personal considerations
- How will proposed outcomes affect my day-to-day life (cash flow, living arrangements, custody schedules)?
- If the respondent is uncooperative, what interim relief can we obtain (temporary orders, injunctions)?
- How will privacy/confidentiality be handled if we settle or if sensitive info becomes part of the court record?

Enforceability & future problems
- How enforceable are likely settlement terms (domestic contempt, garnishment, liens)? What happens if the other party doesn’t pay?
- Can we include modification provisions and under what standard could they be modified later?

Appeals & post-trial
- If we lose at trial, what are the grounds, costs, and timeline for an appeal? Is appeal realistic or advisable?

Document checklist to bring / prepare
- Pleadings and court filings to date (complaint, answer, motions)
- Any proposed settlement offers or correspondence
- Financial documents: recent pay stubs, 3 years of tax returns, bank statements, investment and retirement account statements, credit card statements, loan/mortgage docs
- Property documents: deeds/titles, mortgage statements, appraisal reports, vehicle titles
- Business documents (if applicable): profit/loss, balance sheets, business valuations, corporate filings
- Debt documentation and liabilities
- Household budgets and monthly expenses
- Documents showing income/expenses of the respondent (if available)
- Communications: texts, emails, social media posts relevant to the case
- Photos, videos, or other evidence relevant to custody or claims
- Any prior agreements: prenuptial/postnuptial, separation agreements
- School, medical, or counseling records for children (if custody is involved)
- Witness names and short descriptions of what they can testify about
- Any expert reports already prepared (valuation, custody, etc.)

Status conference prep checklist
- One-page written summary of the case and priorities (for the judge/for your attorney)
- Proposed schedule and a list of outstanding discovery issues
- Settlement posture / whether you want the court to refer the case to mediation
- Any emergency or temporary relief requests to raise now

