I can help you build a clear checklist and list of targeted questions to bring to Robert Kim so you’ll be ready to decide whether to accept a settlement or push for trial. Below is a practical, actionable plan you can use at your next meeting and in the two months before the status conference.

What to bring to your attorney meeting (documents & information)
- Case basics: complaint/petition, summons, any responses, case number, judge assigned, status conference date.
- Identification & marriage info: marriage certificate, separation date(s).
- Financial documents (last 2–3 years where possible):
  - Tax returns (personal & business), W-2s, 1099s
  - Recent pay stubs, employment contracts, bonus/commission documentation
  - Bank statements (all accounts), credit card statements
  - Mortgage statements, deeds, titles, vehicle registrations
  - Retirement & investment account statements (401(k), IRAs, brokerage)
  - Business valuations or profit/loss statements if either spouse owns a business
  - Debts (loans, judgments)
  - Monthly household budget / expense list
- Child-related records (if applicable): birth certificates, custody/parenting proposals, school records, medical bills, daycare costs, special needs documentation.
- Evidence relevant to contested issues: messages/emails, photos, videos, witness names/contacts, proof of misconduct (if relevant to case), proof of contributions to assets.
- Prior offers / communications: any settlement offers exchanged, mediation proposals, written negotiations.
- Expert reports or contacts: appraisers, forensic accountants, custody evaluators, therapists.

What the court may expect at the status conference (typical items)
- Case management & scheduling (deadlines for discovery, motions, mediation)
- Confirmation of parties’ readiness to proceed and outstanding issues
- Whether ADR/mediation has been scheduled or required
- Requests for temporary orders (support or custody) if unresolved
- Possible setting of dates for further hearings or trial
- Requirement to file a joint case management report or disclosures (ask about local form requirements)

Practical timeline (two months → status conference)
- Immediately (this week): schedule a focused meeting with Robert Kim to set priorities; give him the documents above.
- Weeks 1–3: complete outstanding document collection and any mandatory disclosures. Identify witnesses and experts.
- Weeks 3–5: evaluate settlement range (your best and worst outcomes, likely costs/time), consider mediation, prepare a settlement proposal if appropriate.
- 1–2 weeks before status conference: confirm what the court expects you to file (e.g., joint report, docketing statement) and submit those items. Be ready with a concise status update for the judge.
- At the status conference: your attorney will argue scheduling and whether the case is ripe for mediation/trial.

Key questions to ask Robert Kim (direct and decision-focused)
Case posture & likelihood
- How do you evaluate our chances at trial on the main issues (division of property, support, custody)? What are the strongest/weakest facts or evidence?
- What are the realistic best-case and worst-case outcomes (financial and non-financial)?

Costs, time & logistics
- What are the estimated attorney fees and litigation costs if we proceed to trial? How long will trial preparation and the trial itself likely take?
- Are there milestone costs (expert fees, depositions) I should expect soon?

Settlement specifics
- Given the facts, what range of settlement would you recommend (minimum acceptable, ideal, and walk-away points)?
- What non-monetary terms should I prioritize (custody time, decision-making, confidentiality, relocation restrictions)?
- How enforceable are proposed settlement terms in Georgia / Fulton County? What enforcement mechanisms would we include?

Risk assessment & valuation
- What is your assessment of the risks of trial vs. the expected value of settlement? Can you provide a probability-weighted estimate?
- Are there particular evidentiary or legal hurdles that could swing the case at trial?

Negotiation & strategy
- Do you recommend mediation or another ADR before or after the status conference? If yes, when should it be scheduled?
- Who should make the first settlement offer (us or them)? What negotiating strategy do you suggest?
- Can settlement be structured (e.g., lump-sum, installment, property division) to reduce tax or enforcement issues?

Discovery & evidence
- What additional evidence would strengthen our case? Are there specific documents or witnesses we must obtain now?
- Do we need experts (valuation, forensic accountant, custody evaluator)? If so, when should we retain them?

Temporary & interim matters
- Should we seek temporary orders now (support, exclusive use of home, temporary custody)? What is the likelihood of getting them?
- How will temporary orders affect settlement leverage or trial strategy?

Local procedure & status conference specifics
- What does the judge typically expect at a Fulton County civil status conference for divorce matters?
- Are there local forms, deadlines, or mandatory programs (e.g., mandatory mediation, parenting classes) we must follow?

Client decision framework (how to decide)
- Establish your priorities: money, custody, timing, privacy, emotional/career costs.
- Set a clear BATNA (Best Alternative To Negotiated Agreement) — the minimum acceptable settlement you will take.
- Have a quantifiable estimate: expected trial award × probability of success vs. net settlement amount minus fees/time.
- Include non-financial utilities (custody time, confidentiality) — assign them weight in your decision.

What I can do to help next
- Draft a concise meeting agenda or email to Robert Kim summarizing these questions and the documents you’ll bring.
- Create a printable checklist/timeline you can use to track tasks before the status conference.
- Help you craft a negotiation “floor/ceiling” worksheet to bring to your attorney meeting.

Would you like me to draft an email to Robert Kim with an agenda and the documents you plan to provide, or make a printable checklist/timeline you can use right away?

